OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Abelian-covers-Gale-correspondences-and-the-Hodge-conjecture-for-powers-October-6-2026", "title": "Abelian covers, Gale correspondences, and the Hodge conjecture for powers", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Abelian-covers-Gale-correspondences-and-the-Hodge-conjecture-for-powers-October-6-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "032", "family_title": "The rational Hodge conjecture for CM abelian varieties", "abstract_html": "We prove the rational Hodge conjecture on every self-power of the Jacobian at each tensor Hodge-generic point of the full marked variation of a connected abelian cover of curves. This holds in every base genus and for every compatible branching pattern. For any CM abelian variety, the conclusion also holds for every self-power of its product with the Jacobian, on the same Hodge-generic locus. We also prove the conjecture on every self-power of a very general member of the full smooth labelled family of diagonal complete intersections cut out by at most two equations of a common degree, in every dimension and every degree at least two.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Powers of abelian-cover Jacobians — original Theorem 1.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Powers of abelian-cover Jacobians — original Theorem 1.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a compact connected oriented surface \\(B_{\\mathrm{top}}\\), a finite labelled set \\(D\\subset B_{\\mathrm{top}}\\), a point \\(*\\notin D\\), a finite abelian group \\(P\\), and an epimorphism\n  \\[\n  \\varphi:\\pi_1(B_{\\mathrm{top}}\\setminus D,*)\\longrightarrow P\n  \\]\n  whose branch meridians have nonidentity image.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C_{\\mathrm{top}}\\) be the compact covering surface of the associated connected cover, with a chosen point above \\(*\\). Empty branching and the trivial group are allowed.</p><p class=\"li\">Vary the full marked complex structure of the pointed base, including every branch position. Add labelled unbranched auxiliary points only when needed for stability. Let \\(\\mathcal T_\\varphi\\) be the connected marked parameter manifold, let \\(C_s\\) be its covering-curve fibers, and put\n  \\[\n  H_{\\mathbb Q}=H^1(C_{\\mathrm{top}},\\mathbb Q).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(\\mathcal T_\\varphi^{\\mathrm{Hg}}\\) to consist of those \\(s\\in\\mathcal T_\\varphi\\) such that, for every \\(a\\geq0\\), every rational tensor in \\(H_{\\mathbb Q}^{\\otimes 2a}\\) of type \\((a,a)\\) at \\(s\\) has that type throughout \\(\\mathcal T_\\varphi\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A CM abelian variety means a complex abelian variety whose degree-one Hodge group is a torus; a point is allowed.</p><p class=\"li\">The rational Hodge conjecture for a smooth projective complex variety \\(X\\) means that\n  \\[\n  \\mathrm{CH}^p(X)_{\\mathbb Q}\\longrightarrow\n  H^{2p}(X,\\mathbb Q)\\cap H^{p,p}(X,\\mathbb C)\n  \\]\n  is surjective for every \\(p\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(s\\in\\mathcal T_\\varphi^{\\mathrm{Hg}}\\), every complex CM abelian variety \\(M\\), and every integer \\(N\\geq1\\), the rational Hodge conjecture holds in every codimension on\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\operatorname{Jac}(C_s)\\times M\\bigr)^N.\n\\]\nIt also holds on \\(C_s^a\\times M^b\\) for all integers \\(a,b\\geq0\\), where a zeroth power is a point. The point \\(s\\) is chosen before \\(M\\): the same Hodge-generic locus works for every CM companion.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 3 proves that the specified Hodge-generic locus is nonempty and is the complement of a countable union of proper closed analytic tensor loci.</p><p>At every point of that locus, Proposition 9 gives saturated data on \\(\\operatorname{Jac}(C_s)\\). The cited saturated determinant criterion gives the rational Hodge conjecture on every power after adjoining any CM abelian variety \\(M\\). Its application is pointwise after saturation, so \\(M\\) does not enter the definition of \\(\\mathcal T_\\varphi^{\\mathrm{Hg}}\\).</p><p>For the curve-product assertion, algebraic curve–Jacobian correspondences identify first cohomology. A point and the fundamental class supply the other curve degrees.</p><p>Apply the cited correspondence-transfer statement, original Companion results 2.3: if the rational Hodge conjecture holds on every finite product of a source collection \\(\\mathcal S\\), and whole rational cohomology degrees of each target are spanned by algebraic correspondence maps\n\\[\nH^{q-2j}(S,\\mathbb Q)(-j)\\to H^q(Y,\\mathbb Q),\n\\qquad S\\in\\mathcal S,\n\\]\nthen it holds on every finite product of those targets, with repetitions and additional source members. The same applies to \\(k\\)-linear correspondence combinations whose images span after scalar extension.</p><p>External products of the curve–Jacobian maps and point correspondences therefore give the result on every \\(C_s^a\\times M^b\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Powers of diagonal complete intersections — original Theorem 1.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Powers of diagonal complete intersections — original Theorem 1.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(n\\geq2\\), \\(d\\geq0\\), \\(c\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\), and \\(m=d+c+1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a rank-\\(c\\) matrix \\(A=(a_{\\alpha i})\\) with \\(m\\) columns, define\n  \\[\n  X_A=\n  \\left\\{\n  [x_1:\\cdots:x_m]\\in\\mathbb P^{m-1}:\n  \\sum_{i=1}^m a_{\\alpha i}x_i^n=0\n  \\quad(1\\leq\\alpha\\leq c)\n  \\right\\}.\n  \\]\n  Changing the row basis of \\(A\\) does not change \\(X_A\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Retain the coordinate labels and parametrize by the row space of \\(A\\) in\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal D_{c,m}\n  =\n  \\left\\{\n  Q\\in\\operatorname{Gr}(c,m):\n  \\text{every maximal coordinate minor of }Q\\text{ is nonzero}\n  \\right\\}.\n  \\]\n  For \\(c=0\\), the parameter space is a point and \\(X_A=\\mathbb P^{m-1}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Outside a countable union of proper closed algebraic subsets of \\(\\mathcal D_{c,m}\\), the rational Hodge conjecture holds in every codimension on \\(X_A^u\\), for every \\(u\\geq1\\). For \\(c\\leq1\\), or for \\(d=0\\), no exceptional subset is needed.</p><p>This is the full smooth family of labelled diagonal presentations of the indicated rank, not the moduli space of the underlying abstract varieties.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(c=0\\), products of hyperplane classes span the cohomology of every power of projective space. For \\(d=0\\), the smooth target and all its powers are reduced finite sets, supplied by point cycles. Assume \\(d\\geq1\\), \\(c=1\\) or \\(2\\), and put \\(P=\\ker A\\).</p><p>Proposition 13 gives finitely many rational algebraic maps spanning the target cohomology. For a nontrivial character supported on \\(S\\), the source is\n\\[\nF_n^{s-2}\\times Y_S\n\\]\nwith shift \\(-2h_S\\). Apply Proposition 1 to the group \\(H^{s-2}(F_n^{s-2},\\mathbb Q)\\) witnessing character spanning, and compose its finite CM-source maps with the Gale correspondence. These compositions are algebraic, by pullback to a triple product, intersection, and pushforward.</p><p>For finite \\(Y_S\\), inclusions of all its points span \\(H^0(Y_S)\\). For the full-support curve \\(C=X_{P^\\perp}\\), an Abel map \\(C\\to\\operatorname{Jac}(C)\\) gives a degree-preserving pullback isomorphism on \\(H^1\\), represented by its transpose graph. Hyperplane cycles from a point supply the ambient target cohomology.</p><p>Check shifts explicitly. If a Fermat-source map from a CM abelian variety \\(B\\) has shift \\(2j\\), put \\(h=h_S\\), and let \\(A_S=\\operatorname{Jac}(C)\\) when \\(Y_S=C\\), or a point for a chosen finite component otherwise. Composing its degree-preserving map with the Gale shift gives\n\\[\nH^{d+2h-2j}(B\\times A_S,\\mathbb Q)(h-j)\n\\longrightarrow H^d(X_P,\\mathbb Q).\n\\]\nThe witnessing Künneth subspace is\n\\[\nH^{d+h-2j}(B)\\otimes H^h(A_S),\n\\]\nbut the map acts on the entire source degree.</p><p>Put \\(A_C=\\operatorname{Jac}(C)\\) in codimension two and \\(A_C\\) a point in codimension one. In every degree \\(q\\), these maps and ambient point correspondences give a surjection\n\\[\n\\bigoplus_\\nu\nH^{q-2j_\\nu}(A_C^{v_\\nu}\\times B_\\nu,\\mathbb Q)(-j_\\nu)\n\\longrightarrow H^q(X_P,\\mathbb Q),\n\\qquad v_\\nu\\in\\{0,1\\},\n\\]\nwith \\(B_\\nu\\) CM; in codimension one all \\(v_\\nu=0\\). The integers include negative Gale shifts. Scalar-extended character pieces span by Proposition 13. Applying maps to whole rational degrees only enlarges images, so the rational cokernel vanishes. No algebraic Künneth projector is used.</p><p>For \\(c=1\\), take as sources all CM abelian varieties, including a point. Their finite products are CM and satisfy the rational Hodge conjecture by the cited CM Hodge theorem. The correspondence-transfer input stated in the proof of Theorem 1 gives the conclusion on all target powers, at every parameter. Equivalently, each smooth one-equation diagonal hypersurface is coordinate-isomorphic to a Fermat hypersurface.</p><p>For \\(c=2\\), choose \\(P^\\perp\\) outside the exceptional union of Proposition 16. For the resulting fixed curve \\(C\\), use\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_C=\n\\{\\operatorname{Jac}(C)^a\\times M:\na\\geq0,\\ M\\text{ CM}\\}.\n\\]\nFinite products remain of this form. If \\(a\\geq1\\), such a variety is an algebraic retract of\n\\[\n(\\operatorname{Jac}(C)\\times M)^a:\n\\]\nretain all Jacobian factors and one \\(M\\)-factor, and use zero in the remaining \\(M\\)-coordinates for the section. Pull a Hodge class to that power. Proposition 16 makes it algebraic there; pulling back along the section gives an algebraic representative on the retract. For \\(a=0\\), use the CM Hodge theorem. Thus \\(\\mathcal S_C\\) satisfies the required finite-product hypothesis, at the same parameter for every \\(M\\).</p><p>Apply correspondence transfer to the displayed cohomological surjections. This proves the rational Hodge conjecture on every self-power of \\(X_P=X_A\\). The exceptional union depends only on the single dual-curve tensor variation, not on the power or CM sources. Proposition 10 identifies the ambient open with the full smooth diagonal locus.</p><p>The low-dimensional checks of original Remark 8.1 are consistent with the construction. The dual cover has degree \\(n^{m-1}\\), \\(m\\) branch points, and inertia order \\(n\\), so\n\\[\n2g(C)-2=n^{m-2}\\bigl(m(n-1)-2n\\bigr).\n\\]\nFor \\(m=3\\), the original codimension-two target is \\(n^2\\) points and the dual is the three-branch Fermat curve of genus \\((n-1)(n-2)/2\\), with \\(M_3\\) a point. For \\(m=4,n=2\\), the dual is elliptic and retains the full four-point variation. For \\(n=2\\) and odd \\(m\\), the only candidate full-support tuple has odd coordinate sum, so only finite sources occur.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Fermat cohomology supplied by CM abelian varieties — original Proposition 3.3.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Fermat cohomology supplied by CM abelian varieties — original Proposition 3.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">All varieties are complex and cohomology is singular cohomology. Algebraic correspondences have rational coefficients unless otherwise stated.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(k=\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\subset\\mathbb C\\). Scalar automorphisms of \\(k/\\mathbb Q\\) act on coefficients on the fixed complex variety, not on the variety.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the Tate convention\n  \\[\n  V(r)^{p,q}=V^{p+r,q+r},\n  \\qquad\n  \\operatorname{wt}(V(r))=\\operatorname{wt}(V)-2r.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a smooth projective variety \\(X\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal C^q(X)=\n  \\sum_{B,j,\\Gamma}\n  \\operatorname{im}\\!\\left(\n  \\Gamma_*:H^{q-2j}(B,\\mathbb Q)\\longrightarrow H^q(X,\\mathbb Q)\n  \\right),\n  \\]\n  where \\(B\\) ranges over CM abelian varieties, including a point, and\n  \\[\n  \\Gamma\\in\\mathrm{CH}^{\\dim B+j}(B\\times X)_{\\mathbb Q}.\n  \\]\n  A subspace of \\(H^q(X,k)\\) is **CM-supplied** if it lies in \\(\\mathcal C^q(X)\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The cited CM Hodge theorem is [17, Theorem 1.1]; the equivalence with the toric degree-one convention is [17, Lemma 2.3].</p><p class=\"li\">The cited closure statements, original Companion results 2.1(i), are: CM supply is preserved by external products, permutations, cup products, rational algebraic correspondences, \\(k\\)-linear combinations of such correspondences, and scalar conjugation. Every rational Hodge class in a CM-supplied subspace is algebraic.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(n\\geq2\\), \\(a\\geq0\\), put\n  \\[\n  F_n^a=\\{x_0^n+\\cdots+x_{a+1}^n=0\\}\\subset\\mathbb P^{a+1}_{\\mathbb C}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every degree \\(q\\), there are finitely many CM abelian varieties \\(B_\\nu\\), integers \\(j_\\nu\\), and rational algebraic correspondences\n\\[\n\\Gamma_\\nu\\in\n\\mathrm{CH}^{\\dim B_\\nu+j_\\nu}(B_\\nu\\times F_n^a)_{\\mathbb Q}\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\n\\bigoplus_\\nu\nH^{q-2j_\\nu}(B_\\nu,\\mathbb Q)(-j_\\nu)\n\\xrightarrow{\\ \\sum_\\nu(\\Gamma_\\nu)_*\\ }\nH^q(F_n^a,\\mathbb Q)\n\\]\nis surjective. Every \\(B_\\nu\\) may be taken to be a power of the fixed CM abelian variety \\(\\operatorname{Jac}(F_n^1)\\), including its zeroth power. For \\(a=0\\), the reduced finite scheme \\(F_n^0\\) is supplied component by component by points.</p>", "proof": "<p>The Fermat hypersurfaces are smooth. The zero-dimensional assertion is immediate.</p><p>The plane curve \\(F_n^1\\) is connected: distinct positive-degree components in \\(\\mathbb P^2\\) would meet by Bézout, making their union singular. Its coordinate-power map is the quotient by\n\\[\nG=(\\mu_n)^3/\\mu_n\n\\]\nonto \\(z_0+z_1+z_2=0\\), branched at its three coordinate points. By Lemma 1.1, the invariant first cohomology vanishes.</p><p>For a nontrivial character \\(\\chi\\), its summand of the finite pushforward of the constant sheaf is a rank-one local system off the branch points where \\(\\chi\\) is nontrivial on inertia, with zero stalk there; at an inactive point it extends across the point, as is seen by passing to the quotient by \\(\\ker\\chi\\). If there are \\(r\\leq3\\) active points, its Euler characteristic is \\(2-r\\). The connected curve has no nontrivial character in degrees zero or two, so\n\\[\n\\dim_k H^1(F_n^1,k)_\\chi=r-2\\leq1.\n\\]\nEvery character occurring in first cohomology consequently has multiplicity one. The Hodge group of the Jacobian commutes with the algebraic deck action, whose centralizer over \\(k\\) is a product of multiplicative groups. Thus \\(\\operatorname{Jac}(F_n^1)\\) is CM. The Abel map supplies degree one, and point correspondences supply degrees zero and two.</p><p>For \\(a,b\\geq1\\), choose \\(\\varepsilon^n=-1\\). The Shioda–Katsura rational map is\n\\[\nF_n^a\\times F_n^b\\dashrightarrow F_n^{a+b},\n\\qquad\n(x,y)\\longmapsto\n\\bigl((x_i y_{b+1})_{0\\leq i\\leq a},\n(\\varepsilon x_{a+1}y_j)_{0\\leq j\\leq b}\\bigr).\n\\]\nSumming the \\(n\\)th powers gives the target equation. For dominance, take a target point whose first-block power sum is nonzero, rescale it so that this sum is \\(-1\\), and note that the second-block sum is \\(1\\). Set \\(x_{a+1}=y_{b+1}=1\\), take the first block as \\((x_0,\\ldots,x_a)\\), and \\(\\varepsilon^{-1}\\) times the second as \\((y_0,\\ldots,y_b)\\). Source and target have the same dimension, so the map is generically finite.</p><p>Its base locus is\n\\[\nZ=\\{x_{a+1}=y_{b+1}=0\\}\\simeq F_n^{a-1}\\times F_n^{b-1},\n\\]\nsmooth of codimension two. Locally, some \\(x_i\\) and \\(y_j\\) in the displayed blocks are units, so the base ideal is exactly \\((x_{a+1},y_{b+1})\\). Blowing up \\(Z\\) resolves the map to a morphism\n\\[\nf:\\widetilde X\\to F_n^{a+b}.\n\\]</p><p>Induct on target dimension, taking \\(a=1\\), \\(b=d-1\\) in dimension \\(d\\geq2\\). The product \\(F_n^1\\times F_n^{d-1}\\) and the center \\(F_n^0\\times F_n^{d-2}\\) have finite supplies from powers of \\(\\operatorname{Jac}(F_n^1)\\). The possibly disconnected center is handled component by component. Lemma 1.2 supplies \\(\\widetilde X\\). If \\(e&gt;0\\) is the generic degree, then \\(f_*f^*=e\\,\\mathrm{id}\\), so \\(f_*\\) is surjective. Composing supplies with \\(f_*\\) proves the proposition, with the same fixed Jacobian and finitely many maps in each degree.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Finite quotient cohomology — original Lemma 3.1.** Let a finite group \\(G\\) act algebraically on a projective variety \\(V\\), and let \\(q:V\\to V/G\\) be the quotient.", "statement": "<p>**Finite quotient cohomology — original Lemma 3.1.**</p><p>Let a finite group \\(G\\) act algebraically on a projective variety \\(V\\), and let \\(q:V\\to V/G\\) be the quotient. Then\n\\[\nq^*:H^i(V/G,\\mathbb Q)\\xrightarrow{\\sim}H^i(V,\\mathbb Q)^G.\n\\]\nThe assertion remains true after extending coefficients. Pullback is a morphism of mixed Hodge structures. If \\(V\\) is smooth and projective, \\(H^i(V/G,\\mathbb Q)\\) is pure of weight \\(i\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The fibers of \\(q\\) are finite, so proper base change gives \\(R^jq_*\\mathbb Q=0\\) for \\(j&gt;0\\). Each fiber is one \\(G\\)-orbit, hence \\((q_*\\mathbb Q)^G=\\mathbb Q\\). Averaging makes invariants exact, and Leray gives the displayed isomorphism, also after extending coefficients.</p><p>Pullback respects mixed Hodge structures. If \\(V\\) is smooth and projective, strictness of the weight filtration and the injection into \\(H^i(V,\\mathbb Q)\\) give purity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Bundle and blowup supply — original Lemma 3.2.** If the full cohomology of a smooth projective variety \\(S\\) is CM-supplied, the same holds for every projective bundle over \\(S\\).", "statement": "<p>**Bundle and blowup supply — original Lemma 3.2.**</p><p class=\"li\">If the full cohomology of a smooth projective variety \\(S\\) is CM-supplied, the same holds for every projective bundle over \\(S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(Z\\subset X\\) is a nonempty smooth closed subvariety of pure codimension \\(c\\geq1\\) in a smooth projective variety, and the full cohomologies of \\(X\\) and \\(Z\\) are CM-supplied, the same holds for \\(\\operatorname{Bl}_Z X\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In both assertions, finite correspondence supplies for the inputs give finite supplies for the output in each degree.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a projective bundle \\(p:\\mathbb P(E)\\to S\\) of relative dimension \\(e-1\\), put \\(\\xi=c_1(\\mathcal O_{\\mathbb P(E)}(1))\\). The projective-bundle formula is\n\\[\nH^q(\\mathbb P(E),\\mathbb Q)\n=\n\\bigoplus_{i=0}^{e-1}\n\\xi^ip^*H^{q-2i}(S,\\mathbb Q).\n\\]\nThe powers of \\(\\xi\\) restrict to a basis on each fiber, proving the formula by Leray–Hirsch. Every displayed map is algebraic.</p><p>For a blowup \\(b:\\widetilde X\\to X\\), let \\(j:E_Z\\hookrightarrow\\widetilde X\\) and \\(p:E_Z=\\mathbb P(N_{Z/X})\\to Z\\). With \\(\\xi=c_1(\\mathcal O_{E_Z}(1))\\),\n\\[\nH^q(\\widetilde X,\\mathbb Q)\n=\nb^*H^q(X,\\mathbb Q)\n\\oplus\n\\bigoplus_{i=1}^{c-1}\nj_*\\!\\left(\\xi^{i-1}p^*H^{q-2i}(Z,\\mathbb Q)\\right).\n\\]\nThe map of relative pairs \\((\\widetilde X,E_Z)\\to(X,Z)\\) induces an isomorphism in relative cohomology: it is an isomorphism off the closed subsets, and the quotient spaces obtained by collapsing those subsets agree. On \\(E_Z\\), the projective-bundle formula and\n\\[\nj^*j_*\\eta=-\\xi\\eta\n\\]\nshow that subtracting the exceptional terms leaves a class whose restriction comes from \\(Z\\). The long exact sequences of the pairs show that the remaining class comes from \\(b^*H^q(X)\\).</p><p>The sum is direct: \\(b_*b^*=\\mathrm{id}\\), \\(b_*\\) kills the displayed exceptional terms, and their restrictions have independent positive powers of \\(\\xi\\). All maps are algebraic. The CM closure statements give both supply assertions. The formulas have finitely many summands.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Determinants for abelian covers — original Corollary 3.5.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Determinants for abelian covers — original Corollary 3.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let a finite abelian group \\(P\\) act faithfully on a smooth connected projective complex curve \\(C\\), and put \\(B=C/P\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(k=\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\) and inverse pullbacks for the group action.</p><p class=\"li\">For an abelian variety or smooth connected projective curve \\(X\\), a subspace \\(W\\subset H^1(X,k)\\) is **admissible** if it is the image of a \\(k\\)-linear idempotent whose every scalar conjugate preserves the Hodge decomposition. Maps are admissible if they preserve the Hodge decomposition after every scalar conjugation.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\overline W\\) for coefficientwise complex conjugation. A **pair tensor** between \\(W,W'\\) is a tensor in \\(W\\otimes W'\\) inducing \\(W^*\\simeq W'\\), whose every scalar conjugate has type \\((1,1)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(r=\\dim W\\), define on separate curve or abelian-variety slots\n  \\[\n  D(W)=\\bigwedge^rW\\subset W^{\\otimes r}\\subset H^r(X^r,k),\n  \\qquad D(0)=k,\n  \\]\n  with \\(D(0)\\) carried by a point.</p><p class=\"li\">The cited admissible-operations input, original Companion results 2.1(ii), states that admissible maps are induced on the indicated summands by \\(k\\)-linear combinations of rational algebraic correspondences; pair tensors and inverse-pairing contractions are algebraic. Admissible \\(W\\) is paired with \\(\\overline W\\). Alternation factors determinants across admissible direct sums and contracts paired determinants nontrivially; an alternating self-pair supplies its top exterior line.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every character \\(\\chi:P\\to k^\\times\\),\n\\[\nD\\bigl(H^1(C,k)_\\chi\\bigr)\n\\]\nis CM-supplied, including zero character spaces. For \\(\\chi=1\\), the line is generated by a rational algebraic cycle class.</p><p>For \\(\\chi\\neq1\\), let \\(r_\\chi\\) be the number of branch points of \\(C/\\ker\\chi\\to B\\). Then\n\\[\n\\dim_kH^1(C,k)_\\chi=2g(B)+r_\\chi-2.\n\\]\nThese are precisely the original branch points whose inertia is not killed by \\(\\chi\\).</p><p>For fixed \\(P\\), all determinant sources may be taken to be powers of one fixed product of Fermat-curve Jacobians depending only on \\(P\\), not on the complex structure of \\(C\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(\\chi\\neq1\\), put \\(K=\\ker\\chi\\). The quotient \\(C_\\chi=C/K\\) is a smooth connected projective curve: locally a finite stabilizer quotient has parameter \\(t^e\\). The residual cyclic group \\(P/K\\) acts effectively. Indeed, at a point with trivial \\(P\\)-stabilizer, a coset acting trivially on \\(C/K\\) would send that point into its \\(K\\)-orbit and therefore be the identity.</p><p>Lemma 1.1 gives, by quotient pullback,\n\\[\nH^1(C_\\chi,k)_\\chi\\xrightarrow{\\sim}H^1(C,k)_\\chi.\n\\]\nA point of \\(B\\) is branched in \\(C_\\chi\\to B\\) exactly when its original inertia has nontrivial image in \\(P/K\\). Lemma 2.2 supplies its determinant and gives the dimension formula. Tensor powers of the quotient graph pullback transfer the determinant to the original curve slots. Zero spaces give the unit, including quotients that become unramified.</p><p>For \\(\\chi=1\\), quotient pullback identifies the space with \\(H^1(B,k)\\). For a point \\(b\\in B\\),\n\\[\n[\\Delta_B]-[b\\times B]-[B\\times b]\n\\]\nis the \\(H^1(B)\\otimes H^1(B)\\) component of the diagonal and, up to the cup-pairing convention, the inverse symplectic tensor. If \\(g=g(B)\\), antisymmetrizing its \\(g\\)th external power gives a nonzero multiple of its top exterior power, generating \\(D(H^1(B,k))\\) by a rational algebraic class. For \\(g=0\\), it is the unit. Pull back on curve slots.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal O(P)\\) be the finite set of orders of nontrivial characters of \\(P\\). Take\n\\[\nM_P=\\prod_{m\\in\\mathcal O(P)}\\operatorname{Jac}(F_m^1).\n\\]\nLemma 2.2 uses powers of the appropriate factor. Factor inclusions and projections supply each from a power of \\(M_P\\); the trivial character uses a point. This product is CM and depends only on \\(P\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Rank-one local-system vanishing — the elementary vanishing preceding original Lemma 3.4.** A rank-one \\(k\\)-local system on \\((\\mathbb C^\\times)^s\\), nontrivial on at least one circle factor, has zero ordinary and …", "statement": "<p>**Rank-one local-system vanishing — the elementary vanishing preceding original Lemma 3.4.**</p><p>A rank-one \\(k\\)-local system on \\((\\mathbb C^\\times)^s\\), nontrivial on at least one circle factor, has zero ordinary and compactly supported cohomology. The same holds after taking a product with an affine space. A nontrivial rank-one local system on a compact real torus has zero ordinary cohomology.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a circle with monodromy \\(a\\neq1\\), the cellular cochain complex is\n\\[\nk\\xrightarrow{a-1}k,\n\\]\nhence is acyclic. For a product of circles, the cellular complex is the tensor product of the circle complexes. This proves ordinary vanishing on the indicated tori and their products with affine space. Compactly supported vanishing follows by Poincaré duality with the dual local system.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Faithful cyclic determinants — original Lemma 3.4.** Let \\(q:C\\to B\\) be a connected cyclic cover of smooth connected projective complex curves, with group \\(\\Delta\\) of order \\(m\\geq2\\).", "statement": "<p>**Faithful cyclic determinants — original Lemma 3.4.**</p><p>Let \\(q:C\\to B\\) be a connected cyclic cover of smooth connected projective complex curves, with group \\(\\Delta\\) of order \\(m\\geq2\\). Let \\(\\chi:\\Delta\\to k^\\times\\) be faithful and put\n\\[\nV_\\chi=H^1(C,k)_\\chi.\n\\]\nThen \\(D(V_\\chi)\\) is CM-supplied on its product of curve slots, including when \\(V_\\chi=0\\). The sources may all be taken to be powers of the fixed CM abelian variety \\(\\operatorname{Jac}(F_m^1)\\), including a point.</p>", "proof": "<p>The character idempotents and their scalar conjugates preserve the Hodge decomposition, so \\(V_\\chi\\) is admissible. Only the closure properties of CM supply are used until the supply is constructed.</p><p>Let \\(g=g(B)\\), and let \\(D=t_1+\\cdots+t_r\\) be the reduced branch divisor. The branch-meridian images multiply to one in \\(\\Delta\\), so \\(r=0\\) or \\(r\\geq2\\). Faithfulness makes the \\(\\chi\\)-part of \\(q_*k_C\\) a rank-one local system on \\(B\\setminus D\\), with zero stalk at every branch point. Since the nontrivial character does not occur in degrees zero or two,\n\\[\nn:=\\dim_kV_\\chi=2g+r-2.\n\\]\nThere is no nontrivial connected unramified cover of a genus-zero base. Thus \\(n=0\\) occurs only for \\((g,r)=(0,2)\\) or \\((1,0)\\); the determinant is then the unit supplied by a point. Assume \\(n&gt;0\\).</p><p>**The determinant quotient.**\nPut \\(X=C^n\\), \\(G=\\Delta^n\\rtimes\\mathfrak S_n\\), and let \\(N\\) be the kernel of the product homomorphism \\(G\\to\\Delta\\). Form\n\\[\nQ:X\\to Z=X/N,\n\\qquad\n\\pi:Z\\to Z/\\Delta=B^{(n)}=\\operatorname{Sym}^n B.\n\\]\nThe symmetric power is smooth, using elementary symmetric coordinates on each cluster in a small disk. Lemma 1.1 identifies \\(Q^*H^n(Z,k)_\\chi\\) with the subspace on which\n\\[\n(\\delta_1,\\ldots,\\delta_n;\\tau)\n\\]\nacts by \\(\\chi(\\delta_1\\cdots\\delta_n)\\). Each curve slot must have degree one. Geometric permutations include the degree-one Koszul sign, so invariance under \\(\\mathfrak S_n\\) is ordinary antisymmetry. Hence\n\\[\nQ^*H^n(Z,k)_\\chi=D(V_\\chi).\n\\]\nIn particular \\(H^n(Z,k)_\\chi\\) is a line.</p><p>Set \\(U=(B\\setminus D)^{(n)}\\) and \\(\\mathcal L=(\\pi_*k_Z)_\\chi\\). At a divisor meeting a branch point, inertia in the corresponding coordinate has nontrivial product character, so the \\(\\chi\\)-stalk is zero. Over \\(U\\), \\(\\pi\\) is a \\(\\Delta\\)-torsor even on diagonals. Indeed, if a deck tuple followed by a permutation fixes a lifted tuple, the product of deck displacements around every permutation cycle fixes an unramified point and is the identity. The total product is therefore the identity. Locally, sheet labels over disjoint disks, modulo \\(N\\), retain only their total product, giving \\(m\\) local copies of the symmetric-power neighborhood. Thus \\(\\mathcal L\\) is extension by zero of a rank-one local system on \\(U\\).</p><p>Its monodromy is the original cover monodromy evaluated on the sum, with multiplicities, of the moving-point paths in \\(H_1(B\\setminus D,\\mathbb Z)\\). Auxiliary closing paths cancel because \\(\\Delta\\) is abelian. The calculation can be made off the diagonals and extends across them by local triviality.</p><p>Let\n\\[\nu:B^{(n)}\\to\\operatorname{Pic}^nB\n\\]\nbe the Abel map. Its fiber over \\(L\\) is \\(|L|=\\mathbb PH^0(B,L)\\). Put\n\\[\nL_0=K_B(D),\n\\qquad\nT=\\bigl(\\pi^{-1}(|L_0|)\\bigr)_{\\mathrm{red}}.\n\\]\nHere \\(\\deg L_0=n\\).</p><p>**Branched localization.**\nSuppose \\(r&gt;0\\), so \\(n\\geq2g\\). For \\(L\\neq L_0\\), the evaluations\n\\[\nH^0(B,L)\\longrightarrow\\bigoplus_{i=1}^rL|_{t_i}\n\\]\nare surjective. Indeed \\(H^1(B,L)=0\\), and the obstruction is dual to\n\\[\nH^0(B,K_B\\otimes L^{-1}(D)),\n\\]\nwhich vanishes for a nontrivial degree-zero line bundle. Riemann–Roch gives kernel dimension \\(g-1\\). If such an \\(L\\) exists then \\(g\\geq1\\), and coordinates adapted to the evaluations give\n\\[\n|L|\\cap U\\simeq\\mathbb A^{g-1}\\times(\\mathbb C^\\times)^{r-1}.\n\\]\nThe restricted local system is nontrivial. Take a transverse disk through a section on exactly one evaluation hyperplane. Its evaluation has a simple zero. Weierstrass preparation identifies the product of nearby root coordinates with that evaluation times a unit, even for a multiple zero of the section. The sum of winding numbers about the branch point is one, so the moving-divisor rule gives its nontrivial inertia character. Collisions elsewhere can be perturbed apart.</p><p>Lemma 2.1 gives compactly supported vanishing. Since \\(\\mathcal L\\) is extension by zero and \\(|L|\\) is compact, every \\(R^ju_*\\mathcal L\\) is supported at \\(L_0\\). Leray and proper base change therefore give\n\\[\nH^n(Z,k)_\\chi\\xrightarrow{\\sim}H^n(T,k)_\\chi.\n\\]\nFor \\(g=0\\), \\(\\operatorname{Pic}^nB\\) is a point and this is immediate.</p><p>**Fermat sources over the distinguished fiber.**\nThe residue sequence gives\n\\[\n0\\to G_0:=H^0(B,K_B)\n\\to H^0(B,K_B(D))\n\\to R:=\\{(w_1,\\ldots,w_r)\\in\\mathbb C^r:\\sum_iw_i=0\\}\n\\to0.\n\\]\nChoose a vector-space splitting, so \\(|L_0|=\\mathbb P(G_0\\oplus R)\\). Its evaluation hyperplanes are \\(w_i=0\\); they are distinct for \\(r\\geq3\\), and coincide for \\(r=2\\).</p><p>The reduced finite map \\(T\\to|L_0|\\) is still a \\(\\Delta\\)-quotient: averaging commutes with quotienting the defining ideal, and reduction leaves the reduced quotient unchanged. Every component of \\(T\\) dominates \\(|L_0|\\). At least one does because the finite union of their closed images covers the irreducible base. Its translates cover \\(T\\), since each fiber is one \\(\\Delta\\)-orbit.</p><p>Over \\(F=\\mathbb C(|L_0|)\\), the cover is a possibly disconnected \\(\\mu_m\\)-torsor, with algebra \\(F[z]/(z^m-f)\\). It is unramified away from the distinct evaluation hyperplanes \\(H_i=\\{\\ell_i=0\\}\\), \\(1\\leq i\\leq s\\). Valuations at other prime divisors are divisible by \\(m\\). Choose \\(a_i\\), \\(i\\geq2\\), representing the valuations at \\(H_i\\) modulo \\(m\\). The degree-zero condition on a principal divisor gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{div}\\!\\left(\nf\\Big/\\prod_{i=2}^s(\\ell_i/\\ell_1)^{a_i}\n\\right)=mE.\n\\]\nThe Picard group of projective space is torsion-free, so \\(E\\) is principal. Consequently\n\\[\nf=ch^m\\prod_{i=2}^s(\\ell_i/\\ell_1)^{a_i},\n\\qquad c\\in\\mathbb C^\\times,\\quad h\\in F^\\times.\n\\]\nFor \\(r=2\\), \\(s=1\\) and the generic torsor splits.</p><p>The finite surjective coordinate-power map\n\\[\ns_m:F_m^{r-2}\\to\\mathbb PR,\n\\qquad\n[u_1:\\cdots:u_r]\\longmapsto[u_1^m:\\cdots:u_r^m]\n\\]\ngives the smooth projective bundle\n\\[\nY=\n\\mathbb P_{F_m^{r-2}}\n\\left((G_0\\otimes\\mathcal O)\\oplus s_m^*\\mathcal O_{\\mathbb PR}(-1)\\right).\n\\]\nThe tautological line defines a dominant generically finite morphism \\(Y\\to|L_0|\\) on every component. The pulled-back hyperplane ratios are \\((u_i/u_1)^m\\), and \\(c\\) has an \\(m\\)th root. Thus the generic torsor splits over each component's function field, giving rational lifts to every prescribed component of \\(T\\).</p><p>Each lift extends to a morphism: a rational lift from a normal integral variety to a scheme finite over its base sends the finite affine algebra into rational functions integral over every local ring, hence into regular functions. The local lifts glue. Properness makes each image closed and, because it contains the prescribed generic point, surjective onto that component. Taking disjoint copies gives\n\\[\nY'\\to T\n\\]\nproper and surjective, with smooth projective components of dimension \\(g+r-2\\). Proposition 1 and Lemma 1.2 supply their full cohomology. No cohomology of \\(B\\) enters: \\(G_0\\) is a constant vector space. For \\(r=2\\), the components are projective spaces.</p><p>**Unramified localization.**\nSuppose \\(r=0\\). Then \\(g\\geq2\\) and \\(n=2g-2\\). The cover character factors through \\(H_1(B,\\mathbb Z)\\), and defines a nontrivial rank-one local system \\(\\mathcal E\\) on \\(\\operatorname{Pic}^nB\\), after choosing an origin. The moving-divisor rule gives \\(\\mathcal L=u^*\\mathcal E\\).</p><p>Riemann–Roch and Serre duality give\n\\[\nh^0(B,L)=g-1\\quad(L\\neq K_B),\n\\qquad\nh^0(B,K_B)=g.\n\\]\nThe fibers are \\(\\mathbb P^{g-2}\\), except \\(\\mathbb P^{g-1}\\) over \\(K_B\\). Powers of an ample class on \\(B^{(n)}\\) give\n\\[\nk\\simeq R^{2j}u_*k,\\qquad0\\leq j\\leq g-2.\n\\]\nAll odd direct images vanish, and \\(R^{2g-2}u_*k\\) is a rank-one skyscraper at \\(K_B\\). Tensoring with \\(\\mathcal E\\), Lemma 2.1 kills the constant-sheaf terms in Leray. The remaining skyscraper gives the same restriction isomorphism\n\\[\nH^n(Z,k)_\\chi\\simeq H^n(T,k)_\\chi.\n\\]\nThe torsor on \\(|K_B|=\\mathbb P^{g-1}\\) splits because this space is simply connected. Thus \\(T\\) is a disjoint union of projective spaces; take \\(Y'=T\\), supplied by points.</p><p>**Detection and return correspondence.**\nFor a proper surjection \\(Y'\\to T\\) with \\(Y'\\) smooth, Deligne's proper cohomological descent result [5, Proposition 8.2.5] gives\n\\[\n\\ker\\bigl(H^n(T,\\mathbb Q)\\to H^n(Y',\\mathbb Q)\\bigr)\n=\nW_{n-1}H^n(T,\\mathbb Q).\n\\]\nProperness bounds the weights of \\(H^n(T)\\) above by \\(n\\), and the target is pure of weight \\(n\\). The result applies without irreducibility or connectedness assumptions.</p><p>By Lemma 1.1, \\(H^n(Z,\\mathbb Q)\\) is pure. Strictness gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{im}\\bigl(H^n(Z,\\mathbb Q)\\to H^n(T,\\mathbb Q)\\bigr)\n\\cap W_{n-1}H^n(T,\\mathbb Q)=0.\n\\]\nA nonzero \\(\\alpha\\in H^n(Z,k)_\\chi\\) therefore survives on some smooth component \\(Y_0\\) of \\(Y'\\). Let \\(p:Y_0\\to Z\\), \\(d=\\dim Y_0\\). Poincaré duality gives\n\\[\n\\beta\\in H^{2d-n}(Y_0,k),\n\\qquad\n\\int_{Y_0}p^*\\alpha\\smile\\beta\\neq0.\n\\]\nThe pairs \\((d,2d-n)\\) are \\((g+r-2,r-2)\\) in the branched case and \\((g-1,0)\\) in the unramified case. The full cohomology of \\(Y_0\\), hence \\(\\beta\\), is CM-supplied.</p><p>Choose a component of \\(Y_0\\times_ZX\\) dominating \\(Y_0\\) and resolve it projectively. Write\n\\[\nh:\\widetilde Y\\to Y_0,\\qquad l:\\widetilde Y\\to X,\n\\qquad Ql=ph,\n\\]\nwhere \\(h\\) has generic degree \\(e&gt;0\\). The rational cycle\n\\[\n\\Gamma=(h,l)_*[\\widetilde Y]\\in\\mathrm{CH}^n(Y_0\\times X)_{\\mathbb Q}\n\\]\nacts as \\(l_*h^*\\), with shift \\(2(n-d)\\), and sends \\(\\beta\\) to a CM-supplied class in \\(H^n(X,k)\\). No supply for \\(\\widetilde Y\\) is needed. Projection formula gives\n\\[\n\\int_XQ^*\\alpha\\smile\\Gamma_*\\beta\n=\ne\\int_{Y_0}p^*\\alpha\\smile\\beta\\neq0.\n\\]</p><p>Let\n\\[\n\\rho(\\delta_1,\\ldots,\\delta_n;\\tau)=\\chi(\\delta_1\\cdots\\delta_n),\n\\qquad\nP_{\\rho^{-1}}=\\frac1{|G|}\\sum_{a\\in G}\\rho(a)a.\n\\]\nThis is a \\(k\\)-linear combination of algebraic automorphism graphs. Invariance of integration gives the same nonzero pairing after applying \\(P_{\\rho^{-1}}\\) to \\(\\Gamma_*\\beta\\). Its degree-\\(n\\) image is\n\\[\nD(H^1(C,k)_{\\chi^{-1}}).\n\\]\nThat line is CM-supplied. Coefficient conjugation sends \\(\\chi^{-1}\\) to \\(\\chi\\), proving the desired supply.</p><p>All branched sources started from powers of \\(\\operatorname{Jac}(F_m^1)\\), and all later operations merely compose the correspondences. The unramified and zero-rank cases use points. This proves the fixed-source assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**The marked Hodge-generic locus and monodromy — original Lemma 4.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**The marked Hodge-generic locus and monodromy — original Lemma 4.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the full marked cover family in Theorem 1, with base genus \\(h\\), and put\n  \\[\n  H_{\\mathbb Z}=H^1(C_{\\mathrm{top}},\\mathbb Z),\n  \\qquad H_{\\mathbb Q}=H_{\\mathbb Z}\\otimes\\mathbb Q.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Extend \\(\\varphi\\) across any auxiliary punctures by identity meridian images.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Gamma\\) be the stabilizer of the exact epimorphism \\(\\varphi\\) in the pure based mapping class group fixing every label. A chosen point above \\(*\\) determines the lift fixing that point; use inverse pullbacks. These lifts commute with \\(P\\) and act integrally and symplectically.</p><p class=\"li\">For a polarizable rational weight-one Hodge structure \\(H\\) of types \\((1,0),(0,1)\\), let \\(\\operatorname{Hdg}(H)\\) be the smallest rational algebraic subgroup containing the Hodge circle. It is connected reductive and lies in the polarization symplectic group.</p><p class=\"li\">The cited tensor-stabilizer statement, original Companion results 2.1(iii), identifies \\(\\operatorname{Hdg}(H)\\), inside that symplectic group, with the pointwise stabilizer of all rational tensors of type \\((a,a)\\) in \\(H^{\\otimes2a}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L\\) be the rational Zariski closure of \\(\\Gamma\\), and put \\(J_s=\\operatorname{Hdg}(H_s)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(a\\geq0\\), \\(t\\in H_{\\mathbb Q}^{\\otimes2a}\\), define\n\\[\nZ_t=\\{s\\in\\mathcal T_\\varphi:t_{\\mathbb C}\\in F^a(H_{\\mathbb C}^{\\otimes2a})_s\\}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_\\varphi^{\\mathrm{Hg}}\n=\n\\mathcal T_\\varphi\\setminus\n\\bigcup_{\\substack{a\\geq0,\\ t\\in H_{\\mathbb Q}^{\\otimes2a}\\\\Z_t\\neq\\mathcal T_\\varphi}}Z_t\n\\]\nis nonempty, and each \\(Z_t\\) is closed complex analytic. At every \\(s\\in\\mathcal T_\\varphi^{\\mathrm{Hg}}\\),\n\\[\nL^\\circ\\subset J_s,\n\\]\nand the whole group \\(\\Gamma\\) normalizes \\(J_s\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The group \\(\\Gamma\\) has finite index, since there are finitely many homomorphisms from the finitely generated surface group to \\(P\\). The marked variation is obtained by lifting charts off \\(D\\) and filling with the fixed local power charts; auxiliary marks do not change its curves or cohomology.</p><p>A rational tensor is real. In weight \\(2a\\), membership in \\(F^a\\) is equivalent to type \\((a,a)\\), because reality also places it in \\(\\overline{F^a}\\). Membership in the holomorphic subbundle \\(F^a\\) is the vanishing of the induced quotient-bundle section. Thus \\(Z_t\\) is closed analytic. There are countably many rational tensors. Proper analytic subsets of a connected complex manifold are nowhere dense, so Baire's theorem gives nonemptiness. Odd tensor degrees have no balanced vectors, since the circle element \\(-1\\) acts by \\(-1\\).</p><p>At a point of the complement, rational balanced tensors are exactly the tensors balanced throughout the marked family. Their spaces are \\(\\Gamma\\)-stable by equivariance. Their pointwise stabilizer is \\(J_s\\), so \\(\\Gamma\\) normalizes \\(J_s\\).</p><p>A globally balanced tensor has finite \\(\\Gamma\\)-orbit. If its denominator is \\(q\\), every translate lies in \\(q^{-1}H_{\\mathbb Z}^{\\otimes2a}\\). Its tensor-polarization norm is unchanged. At a fixed fiber, all translates lie in the real balanced subspace, where the tensor polarization, with its fixed weight sign, is definite. A fixed norm sphere is compact and contains only finitely many lattice points. Therefore \\(L^\\circ\\) fixes every globally balanced tensor, and the tensor-stabilizer statement gives \\(L^\\circ\\subset J_s\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Rank-one nilpotent criterion — original Lemma 4.3.** Let \\(V\\) be a finite-dimensional vector space over \\(k=\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\), and let a connected reductive algebraic subgroup of \\(\\operatorname{GL}(V)\\) act …", "statement": "<p>**Rank-one nilpotent criterion — original Lemma 4.3.**</p><p>Let \\(V\\) be a finite-dimensional vector space over \\(k=\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\), and let a connected reductive algebraic subgroup of \\(\\operatorname{GL}(V)\\) act irreducibly. If its Lie algebra contains a rank-one nilpotent, its derived image is the standard special linear group or the standard symplectic group on \\(V\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>It is enough to prove the Lie algebra assertion, since the asserted groups are connected. Extend scalars to \\(\\mathbb C\\). In the symplectic alternative, the invariant alternating form descends to \\(k\\): invariant forms solve linear equations over \\(k\\), and a nondegenerate alternating solution can be chosen over \\(k\\). Equality of connected group images descends.</p><p>The center acts by scalars. Jordan decomposition shows that a nilpotent has zero central component and nilpotent components in the simple ideals. Write the irreducible semisimple representation as \\(\\bigotimes_iV_i\\). For the rank-one nilpotent \\(N\\), \\(N^2=0\\). If two components \\(N_i,N_j\\) were nonzero, choose length-two terminal Jordan strings in those factors and kernel vectors elsewhere. On the tensor product of the top vectors,\n\\[\nN^2=2N_i\\otimes N_j\\neq0,\n\\]\na contradiction. If only \\(N_i\\) is nonzero, then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}N\n=\n\\operatorname{rank}(N_i)\\prod_{j\\neq i}\\dim V_j.\n\\]\nThus the semisimple image is simple.</p><p>Put \\(N\\) in a Borel nilradical. A generic one-parameter subgroup of its maximal torus, with scalar rescaling, degenerates \\(N\\) to a nonzero root vector \\(x_\\alpha\\). Rank at most one is closed, so \\(x_\\alpha\\) has rank one. Every root vector of that root length has rank one by Weyl conjugacy. Restriction to the root \\(\\mathfrak{sl}_2\\) is one standard doublet and trivial representations, because the raising-operator rank on an irreducible \\(a\\)-dimensional \\(\\mathfrak{sl}_2\\)-module is \\(a-1\\). Each corresponding reflection therefore exchanges precisely two weights, both of multiplicity one, and fixes all other weights.</p><p>Use the classification and weight lattices of reduced irreducible root systems. Roots of a specified length form a full-rank simply laced root system: the entire system in simply laced type; \\(D_\\ell\\) or \\(A_1^\\ell\\) in types \\(B_\\ell,C_\\ell\\); \\(D_4\\) in \\(F_4\\); and \\(A_2\\) in \\(G_2\\). Use \\(D_3=A_3\\), \\(D_2=A_1\\sqcup A_1\\).</p><p>Its simple reflections are distinct transpositions on the weights, since weights span the dual Cartan. Orthogonal roots give disjoint transpositions; adjacent roots give transpositions sharing one endpoint. A branching vertex would require three disjoint transpositions each meeting the same two-element set, which is impossible. A connected full-rank path \\(A_\\ell\\) acts on exactly \\(\\ell+1\\) moved weights by successive path transpositions. All other weights are zero. The moved weights form the standard regular simplex; their successive differences are the simple roots with consistent sign, as follows by pairing with adjacent coroots. In full type \\(A_\\ell\\), the highest weight is the first or last fundamental weight, giving the standard representation or its dual. If the path arose from \\(G_2,B_3,C_3\\), the full Weyl group contains negation, whereas this simplex for \\(\\ell\\geq2\\) is not stable under negation.</p><p>For \\(A_1^\\ell\\), the transpositions are disjoint. A weight moved by one axis reflection is fixed by all others, hence lies on that axis, and the doublet condition gives \\(\\pm\\alpha/2\\). For long roots \\(2e_i\\) in \\(C_\\ell\\), the weights are \\(\\pm e_i\\), giving the symplectic standard module. For short roots \\(e_i\\) in \\(B_\\ell\\), \\(\\pm e_i/2\\) are not weights for \\(\\ell\\geq2\\). For rank-two \\(D_2\\), the half-roots \\((\\pm e_1\\pm e_2)/2\\) are not weights of \\(C_2\\); in \\(B_2\\) they give the spin representation, standard under \\(B_2=C_2\\). Rank one gives the standard \\(\\mathfrak{sl}_2\\)-module. These are exactly the asserted alternatives.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Character cocycles, twists, and connected irreducibility — original Lemmas 4.4 and 4.5.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Character cocycles, twists, and connected irreducibility — original Lemmas 4.4 and 4.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For a character \\(\\chi:P\\to k^\\times\\) of a fixed topological abelian cover, put\n  \\[\n  W_\\chi=H^1(C,k)_\\chi.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\chi\\neq1\\), pass to the faithful cyclic quotient and fill punctures with multiplier one. Choose the convention in which \\(W_\\chi\\) is computed by \\(t=\\chi\\varphi\\). Let \\(t_1,\\ldots,t_r\\neq1\\) be its active local multipliers.</p><p class=\"li\">Use handle generators \\(a_j,b_j\\) and positively based meridians \\(g_i\\), with\n  \\[\n  \\prod_j[a_j,b_j]\\prod_i g_i=1.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The cocycle model, twist formula, Hermitian fixed-hyperplane and irreducibility criteria, and connected-irreducibility criterion in Lemmas 5.1 and 5.2 below hold. In particular,\n\\[\nW_1=H^1(B,k),\n\\qquad\n\\dim W_\\chi=2h+r-2\\quad(\\chi\\neq1).\n\\]\nThese conclusions are available for the subsequent positive-genus and sphere character calculations.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 5.1 gives the character-cohomology and twist calculations and the direction-graph criterion. The invariant-character identification follows from finite quotient cohomology. Lemma 5.2 gives the connected-irreducibility conclusion for the twist groups whenever a rank-one Lie nilpotent is present.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Cocycles and twists — original Lemma 4.4.** In the setting of Proposition 5, \\(W_\\chi\\) for nontrivial \\(t\\) is the space of crossed homomorphisms …", "statement": "<p>**Cocycles and twists — original Lemma 4.4.**</p><p>In the setting of Proposition 5, \\(W_\\chi\\) for nontrivial \\(t\\) is the space of crossed homomorphisms\n\\[\nz(uv)=z(u)+t(u)z(v),\n\\qquad\nz(u)\\sim z(u)+(1-t(u))b,\\quad b\\in k,\n\\]\nsubject to the relation on the displayed surface word.</p><p>For an embedded arc \\(\\alpha\\) between two active punctures, a boundary twist of its two-puncture disk acts, in adapted coordinates and one twist convention, by\n\\[\nT_\\alpha=1+u_\\alpha\\ell_\\alpha,\n\\quad\nu_\\alpha=(-t_i,1),\n\\quad\n\\ell_\\alpha(z)=(1-t_j)z(g_i)+(t_i-1)z(g_j).\n\\]\nThe vector is supported on those meridians; the exceptional eigenvalue is \\(t_it_j\\). A different lift can multiply this formula by a finite-order scalar.</p><p>For a nontrivial such transformation, its fixed hyperplane is the Hermitian orthogonal complement of its image direction. A collection with connected nonorthogonality graph and zero intersection of fixed hyperplanes acts irreducibly. The same statements apply to transvections from simple closed curves with trivial \\(t\\)-monodromy.</p>", "proof": "<p>Averaging over the finite deck group identifies the eigenspace with rank-one local-system cohomology on the punctured base. Filling inactive punctures gives ordinary cohomology there. At an active puncture the local complex\n\\[\nk\\xrightarrow{t_i-1}k\n\\]\nis acyclic. The cellular or crossed-homomorphism model gives the stated presentation. Euler characteristic gives \\(\\dim W_\\chi=2h+r-2\\); in the unramified nontrivial case, degrees zero and two vanish.</p><p>Conjugate the two meridians by \\(\\delta=g_ig_j\\), fixing exterior generators. The identity\n\\[\nz(fuf^{-1})=(1-t(u))z(f)+t(f)z(u)\n\\]\ngives the twist formula by subtraction, and\n\\[\n\\ell_\\alpha(u_\\alpha)=t_it_j-1.\n\\]\nDisk twists preserve \\(\\varphi\\), acting trivially on abelianization. For other mapping classes preserving \\(t\\), a positive power preserves \\(\\varphi\\), which suffices for Lie-algebra statements.</p><p>Put \\(s_i=z(g_i)/(1-t_i)\\). The fixed-hyperplane condition is equality of these affine fixed points after transport. If the arc is represented by \\(p_i^{-1}xp_j\\), it is\n\\[\ns_i=z(x)+t(x)s_j.\n\\]\nEndpoint windings do not change this description. For \\(r\\geq3\\), the image vector is nonzero modulo coboundaries because another active meridian has zero coordinate. For \\(r=2\\), if it is a coboundary, every exterior multiplier is one, and the surface relation also makes the functional zero.</p><p>The polarization pairs opposite character spaces perfectly and gives a nondegenerate monodromy-invariant Hermitian form. For a nontrivial isometry \\(T=1+u\\ell\\), every fixed vector is orthogonal to \\(ku=(T-1)W\\); both are codimension-one conditions, proving the assertion. Zero common fixed space makes the image directions span. A nonzero invariant subspace contains a vector moved by some transformation, hence its image direction; nonorthogonality propagates this to every direction.</p><p>For a simple closed curve with trivial \\(t\\)-monodromy, lift its annulus to the cyclic cover and twist simultaneously around its lifts. The intersection formula has rank at most one on a character space because the projected lift classes are scalar multiples. Nonzero cocycle evaluation on a closed lift gives a nonzero intersection-dual projected class by the perfect opposite-character pairing. The twist is then a nontrivial rank-one unipotent. Its logarithm, or a nonzero integral multiple after taking a power, lies in connected monodromy's Lie algebra.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Connected irreducibility — original Lemma 4.5.** Let a subgroup of \\(\\operatorname{GL}(V)\\) act irreducibly and be generated, up to scalars, by rank-one changes of the identity.", "statement": "<p>**Connected irreducibility — original Lemma 4.5.**</p><p>Let a subgroup of \\(\\operatorname{GL}(V)\\) act irreducibly and be generated, up to scalars, by rank-one changes of the identity. If the Lie algebra of its Zariski closure contains a rank-one nilpotent, the identity component of that closure acts irreducibly.</p>", "proof": "<p>The unipotent radical of the identity component is normal in the full closure. Its common fixed space is nonzero and full-group invariant, hence all of \\(V\\); the radical is trivial in the faithful image. The identity component is reductive, and the full group permutes its isotypic summands transitively.</p><p>A rank-one nilpotent acts nontrivially on only one isotypic summand and there with multiplicity one; that summand has dimension at least two. Transitivity gives the same properties for every summand. A scalar multiple of a rank-one change cannot permute two summands of dimension at least two, since its difference from that scalar identity would have rank at least two. Every generator therefore preserves every summand. Full-group irreducibility forces a single summand of multiplicity one.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Positive-genus character projections — original Proposition 4.7.** Let the base genus be \\(h\\geq1\\), and use the full marked variation of Theorem 1.", "statement": "<p>**Positive-genus character projections — original Proposition 4.7.**</p><p>Let the base genus be \\(h\\geq1\\), and use the full marked variation of Theorem 1. At every \\(s\\in\\mathcal T_\\varphi^{\\mathrm{Hg}}\\):</p><p class=\"li\">Every nonzero nontrivial character space \\(W_\\chi\\) is mixed, meaning that both its \\((1,0)\\) and \\((0,1)\\) parts are nonzero, at every scalar conjugate.</p><p class=\"li\">The derived Hodge image on \\(W_\\chi\\) is the full standard special linear or full standard symplectic group.</p><p class=\"li\">The invariant space, when nonzero, has Hodge dimensions \\((h,h)\\) and full standard symplectic derived action.</p><p>The handle character may be put in the form\n\\[\nt(a_j)=t(b_j)=1\\quad(j&lt;h),\n\\qquad t(b_h)=1,\\qquad t(a_h)=\\eta.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>To obtain the handle normal form, lift the character on handle homology to an integral row, divide by the greatest common divisor of its entries, and complete the primitive part to an integral symplectic basis. Realize the basis change by a homeomorphism supported on the handle subsurface. If all handle multipliers are one, take \\(\\eta=1\\). This changes the marking, not the cover.</p><p>We exhibit an irreducible twist group and a rank-one nilpotent in the Lie algebra of its connected closure.</p><p>**No active punctures.**\nThen \\(\\eta\\neq1\\). The surface relation gives\n\\[\n(\\eta-1)z(b_h)=0.\n\\]\nThus \\(z(b_h)=0\\), and a coboundary removes \\(z(a_h)\\). The remaining coordinates are ordinary first-cohomology coordinates of the first \\(h-1\\) handles. Twists along a symplectic handle basis and connecting band sums give rank-one transvections with spanning directions and connected nonorthogonality graph. Positive powers preserving \\(\\varphi\\) have the same logarithm directions, and connected irreducibility follows. For \\(h=1\\), the character space is zero.</p><p>**At least three active punctures.**\nConsecutive puncture-disk arcs force all \\(s_i\\) equal in their common fixed space. Adjacent directions pair nontrivially by the twist formula; the factors are nonzero multiples of \\(t_j,1-t_i,1-t_k\\). Add winding arcs from Lemma 6.1 for every handle generator. Each is linked to the standard \\(2,3\\) direction in their three-puncture neighborhood. Subtract a coboundary to make all \\(s_i=0\\); the winding conditions then make all handle cocycle values zero. Thus the common fixed space is zero and the direction graph is connected, giving irreducibility by Proposition 5.</p><p>The twist about \\(b_h\\), replacing \\(a_h\\) by \\(a_hb_h\\), has displacement supported on \\(a_h\\) with value \\(\\eta z(b_h)\\). The coordinate \\(z(b_h)\\) is free, with the surface relation compensated at an active meridian. The image vector is not a coboundary because it vanishes on active meridians. This is a nonzero square-zero rank-one operator. A positive power preserves \\(\\varphi\\), and a further power removes any deck scalar. Adjoining it preserves irreducibility and the rank-one-generation property. Proposition 5 gives connected irreducibility.</p><p>**Two active punctures.**\nWrite \\(t_1=\\lambda\\), \\(t_2=\\lambda^{-1}\\). The relation is\n\\[\n(\\eta-1)z(b_h)+(1-\\lambda)(s_1-s_2)=0.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\eta=1\\), remove the common value \\(s_1=s_2\\) by a coboundary. All handle coordinates are ordinary symplectic coordinates, and handle twists and band sums apply.</p><p>Suppose \\(\\eta\\neq1\\), and impose \\(s_2=0\\). Handle coordinates are arbitrary and determine \\(s_1\\). Use the standard \\(1,2\\) arc and winding arcs for \\(a_h\\) and for \\(a_j,b_j\\), \\(j&lt;h\\). Their fixed equations are \\(s_1=0\\) and \\(s_1=z(x)\\), after possible reversal. Reversal changes a trivial-multiplier coordinate by sign and the \\(a_h\\)-coordinate by \\(-\\eta^{-1}\\), which does not affect the argument. The common kernel is zero.</p><p>Before gauging, the standard-arc direction has zero handle coordinates and\n\\[\ns_1=s_2=-\\lambda/(1-\\lambda).\n\\]\nAdding the coboundary with parameter \\(\\lambda/(1-\\lambda)\\) yields\n\\[\nz(a_h)=\\frac{(1-\\eta)\\lambda}{1-\\lambda}\\neq0,\n\\qquad z(b_h)=s_1=0,\n\\]\nand all other handle coordinates zero. Thus the \\(a_h\\)-winding functional is nonzero there. Hermitian symmetry gives reciprocal nonzero pairing, so the \\(a_h\\)-winding direction has \\(s_1\\neq0\\). It has zero first-\\(h-1\\)-handle coordinates by Lemma 6.1; gauging does not change these trivial-multiplier coordinates. Every other winding functional is consequently nonzero on it. The graph is connected. All pair products are one, so the nontrivial pair twists are rank-one unipotents. Proposition 5 gives connected irreducibility.</p><p>In every case the connected twist closure is irreducible and has a rank-one Lie nilpotent. By Proposition 3 it lies in the projected Hodge group at \\(s\\). That group is reductive and irreducible; Proposition 4 gives the standard special linear or symplectic derived image.</p><p>All constructions survive scalar conjugation: active sets and all displayed nonzero factors remain unchanged in the required sense. Every conjugate therefore has a nontrivial unipotent preserving its Hermitian polarization. A definite Hermitian space has no such unipotent isometry, whereas a pure character space has definite polarization. Thus every conjugate is mixed.</p><p>For the invariant character, ordinary handle transvections, after powers in the stabilizer, give the full symplectic group by the same direction argument and Proposition 4. The restricted polarization rules out a larger special linear alternative in dimension greater than two.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Winding arcs — original Lemma 4.6.** Place the active punctures in an ordered disk away from the handles.", "statement": "<p>**Winding arcs — original Lemma 4.6.**</p><p>Place the active punctures in an ordered disk away from the handles. For every simple based handle generator \\(x\\), an embedded arc from puncture \\(1\\) to puncture \\(2\\) can be chosen whose transported fixed-point condition is\n\\[\ns_1=z(x)+t(x)s_2\n\\]\nwith \\(x\\) or \\(x^{-1}\\). If \\(r\\geq3\\), the arc may avoid the interior of the standard \\(2,3\\) arc. Arcs for different generators are chosen separately.</p><p>For a winding arc using only the last handle, simple generators of the first \\(h-1\\) handles can be represented outside its two-puncture disk.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take the based handle bouquet on one side of a small vertex disk and fan the puncture access paths \\(p_i\\) on the other. Choose the first two paths to avoid the standard \\(2,3\\) arc when present. Concatenate \\(p_1^{-1}xp_2\\). Outside the vertex disk its pieces are embedded and disjoint. Choose the orientation of \\(x\\) so that the two puncture-side and two handle-side germs can be joined in noncrossing pairs. Smoothing gives the embedded arc. Its neighborhood with the endpoint disks is a disk; after adding the \\(2,3\\) arc, the neighborhood of the three-vertex tree is also a disk. Thus consecutive-meridian formulas apply. Auxiliary marks are avoided by perturbation.</p><p>For the last assertion, use separated handles: place the winding arc in the last-handle and puncture region, with the other loops outside. Carrying choices by the homeomorphism giving the handle normal form preserves this. Changing based access paths conjugates the loops; because their multipliers are one, zero displacement remains zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Sphere character projections — original Proposition 4.11.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Sphere character projections — original Proposition 4.11.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The base is a sphere. Let \\(\\chi\\neq1\\), and discard inactive punctures in computing\n  \\[\n  W_\\chi=H^1(C,k)_\\chi.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Its active multipliers satisfy\n  \\[\n  t_i\\neq1,\\qquad\\prod_{i=1}^rt_i=1,\n  \\qquad n:=\\dim_kW_\\chi=r-2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M_\\chi\\) be the Zariski closure of the \\(\\Gamma\\)-action on \\(W_\\chi\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(s\\in\\mathcal T_\\varphi^{\\mathrm{Hg}}\\), put \\(J=J_s\\), and call a character space non-toric if \\(J^{\\mathrm{der}}\\) acts nontrivially on it.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(n\\geq3\\), either \\(M_\\chi\\) is finite and the Hodge action on the rational scalar orbit of \\(W_\\chi\\) is toric, or\n\\[\nM_\\chi^\\circ=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\operatorname{Sp}(W_\\chi),&amp;\\chi^2=1,\\\\\n\\operatorname{SL}(W_\\chi),&amp;\\chi^2\\neq1.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nIn the infinite case, the derived Hodge projection is the same standard group.</p><p>In dimensions at most two, every non-toric character space has dimension two and standard derived Hodge projection \\(\\operatorname{SL}_2\\).</p><p>Each non-toric character space is supported by a single simple ideal of \\(\\operatorname{Lie}(J^{\\mathrm{der}})_k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(n\\geq3\\), Lemma 7.3 and Proposition 4 give full standard special linear or symplectic derived algebra of connected monodromy. Lemma 7.1 makes its connected scalar center trivial.</p><p>In the symplectic case, all of \\(M_\\chi\\) normalizes its connected symplectic group and preserves the unique invariant alternating form up to scalar. For dimension at least four, a semisimple pseudoreflection of ratio different from one cannot be such a similitude. Normalize its fixed hyperplane \\(U\\) to eigenvalue one. Since \\(\\dim U=n-1&gt;n/2\\), the alternating form does not vanish identically on \\(U\\), so the multiplier is one. The exceptional line would be orthogonal to \\(U\\) and itself, hence radical, a contradiction. Every active pair product is therefore one. At least three active points force all \\(t_i=-1\\), and sphere meridians generate the deck group, so \\(\\chi\\) has order two. Conversely, an order-two space has restricted nondegenerate alternating polarization and nontrivial unipotent pair twists, giving the symplectic alternative.</p><p>The algebraic image of \\(L_k\\) on \\(W_\\chi\\) is \\(M_\\chi\\), and that of \\(L_k^\\circ\\) is \\(M_\\chi^\\circ\\). Proposition 3 puts the latter inside the Hodge image. Full traceless algebra in the linear case, and the restricted alternating polarization in the order-two case, give exactly the asserted derived Hodge projections. Finite monodromy gives the toric alternative by Lemma 7.2.</p><p>A connected reductive subgroup of \\(\\operatorname{GL}_2\\) either acts torically or has full standard \\(\\operatorname{SL}_2\\) derived image: a reducible representation is a sum of characters, and the only nonzero semisimple subalgebra of \\(\\mathfrak{sl}_2\\) is \\(\\mathfrak{sl}_2\\). Dimension one is toric and zero is omitted.</p><p>Each displayed derived image is simple. A surjection from a semisimple Lie algebra to a simple algebra is supported on exactly one simple ideal.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Finite determinant image and scalar orbits — original Lemma 4.8.** In the sphere setting of Proposition 7:", "statement": "<p>**Finite determinant image and scalar orbits — original Lemma 4.8.**</p><p>In the sphere setting of Proposition 7:</p><p class=\"li\">The determinant character of \\(M_\\chi\\) has finite image.</p><p class=\"li\">If monodromy on one scalar character space is finite, monodromy on the rational sum of its scalar orbit is finite.</p><p class=\"li\">If the Hermitian forms on every member of a scalar orbit are definite, monodromy on that rational sum is finite.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a cyclotomic number field \\(E\\) containing the character values. The projector and representation are defined over \\(E\\). The determinant of an integral monodromy operator on the character image is an algebraic integer, since its eigenvalues occur in integral first cohomology. Applying this to the inverse makes it a unit. At every embedding of \\(E\\), the operator preserves a nondegenerate Hermitian form, so its determinant has absolute value one. An algebraic integer all of whose conjugates have absolute value one is a root of unity: the monic polynomials with roots the conjugates of its positive powers have bounded integral coefficients, and their finite number forces two powers to coincide. There are finitely many roots of unity in \\(E\\), and this set is Zariski closed. The determinant image of \\(M_\\chi\\) is finite.</p><p>Matrices at other embeddings are obtained by applying those embeddings to the \\(E\\)-matrices. A finite matrix set remains finite, proving the scalar-orbit assertion.</p><p>If every translated Hermitian form is definite, change negative signs and sum them. Restriction from the complexification gives an invariant positive definite real form on the rational orbit representation. It has an invariant lattice obtained by intersection with integral first cohomology. Every lattice basis vector has finitely many possible images of its fixed length. Thus monodromy is finite.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Finite monodromy and purity — original Lemma 4.9.** Assume \\(n\\geq2\\) in Proposition 7.", "statement": "<p>**Finite monodromy and purity — original Lemma 4.9.**</p><p>Assume \\(n\\geq2\\) in Proposition 7. If \\(M_\\chi\\) is finite, every scalar translate of \\(W_\\chi\\) is pure at every fiber. At every fiber, its rational Hodge group acts by scalars on each character space in the orbit and torically on their rational sum.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(V_{\\mathbb Q}\\) be the rational character-orbit summand. The orbit contains the inverse character, so polarization restricts nondegenerately. It is a polarized subvariation with finite monodromy by Lemma 7.1.</p><p>Since \\(r\\geq4\\), the ordered marked-sphere configuration space modulo projective coordinates is smooth quasi-projective; fix three labels and include the labelled base point and its chosen lift. The finite-index stabilizer of \\(\\varphi\\) gives a finite topological cover, algebraic finite étale by Riemann existence. The base-point section splits the fundamental-group sequence of the universal punctured curve: it kills the homotopy boundary map into the fiber group and splits projection. The preserved epimorphism \\(\\varphi\\), together with identity images for base loops, therefore defines the finite cover of the total punctured curve. Riemann existence makes it algebraic.</p><p>Normalize the universal compact curve in this cover. Locally near branch sections its analytic charts are\n\\[\n(u,s)\\longmapsto(u^e,s).\n\\]\nIt is a smooth proper cover family and a quotient of the full marked variation. The lifted base-point section fixes the sheet, so its monodromy is the specified \\(\\Gamma\\)-action. Pullback to \\(\\mathcal T_\\varphi\\) is the original family, with the same epimorphism, sheet, and local power charts.</p><p>Pass to the finite étale cover killing monodromy of \\(V_{\\mathbb Q}\\). Its period map is single-valued into a Siegel domain, realized by a Cayley transform as a bounded domain. Coordinate functions are bounded holomorphic functions. On a smooth projective compactification with normal-crossing boundary, removable singularities extend them across local boundary polydisks. They are constant on the compactification. Thus the period map is constant.</p><p>If a character were mixed, pass to the faithful cyclic quotient\n\\[\n\\pi:Y=C/\\ker\\chi\\to\\mathbb P^1\n\\]\nwith active branch divisor \\(D_\\chi\\). Forgetting inactive and auxiliary marks is locally a submersion, so it suffices to vary these active branch points.</p><p>In a branch chart \\(x=u^e\\), an invariant vector field is\n\\[\nu\\,b(u^e)\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u},\n\\]\nand pushes forward to \\(exb(x)\\partial/\\partial x\\). Therefore\n\\[\n(\\pi_*T_Y)^{P/\\ker\\chi}=T_{\\mathbb P^1}(-D_\\chi),\n\\qquad\nH^1(Y,T_Y)^{P/\\ker\\chi}\n=\nH^1(\\mathbb P^1,T_{\\mathbb P^1}(-D_\\chi)).\n\\]\nThe branch-variation tangent map is this identification: differentiating lifted Čech coordinate changes gives the inverse pushforward. Since \\(r\\geq4\\), the pointed base has no infinitesimal automorphisms. This does not require \\(H^0(Y,T_Y)=0\\).</p><p>Mixedness gives nonzero\n\\[\n\\alpha\\in H^0(Y,K_Y)_\\chi,\n\\qquad\n\\beta\\in H^0(Y,K_Y)_{\\chi^{-1}}.\n\\]\nTheir product is a nonzero invariant quadratic differential and pairs nontrivially with some invariant deformation by Serre duality and averaging. Equivalently, invariant quadratic differentials descend to\n\\[\nH^0(\\mathbb P^1,K_{\\mathbb P^1}^{\\otimes2}(D_\\chi)),\n\\]\ndual to the preceding tangent space: local invariance allows at most a simple pole downstairs, whose pullback is holomorphic.</p><p>For a vector-field deformation cocycle \\(\\theta_{ij}\\), Cartan's formula gives first-order differences\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{\\theta_{ij}}\\alpha=d(\\theta_{ij}\\lrcorner\\alpha).\n\\]\nThe period variation modulo holomorphic forms is represented in \\(H^1(Y,\\mathcal O_Y)\\) by \\(\\theta_{ij}\\lrcorner\\alpha\\), up to a common sign. Pairing with \\(\\beta\\) evaluates the deformation on \\(\\alpha\\beta\\), nonzero for the chosen tangent. This works also in genus one and contradicts constant period. Every scalar translate is therefore pure.</p><p>Let \\(F=\\mathbb Q(\\chi(P))\\). The rational orbit is an \\(F\\)-vector space whose embedding spaces are the character spaces. Their purity makes the Hodge circle scalar on each, so it centralizes \\(\\operatorname{End}_F(V_{\\mathbb Q})\\). The rational Hodge group centralizes the same algebra and lies in the scalar torus \\(\\operatorname{Res}_{F/\\mathbb Q}\\mathbb G_m\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Infinite sphere monodromy and rank-one Lie elements — original Lemma 4.10.** Assume \\(n\\geq3\\) in Proposition 7.", "statement": "<p>**Infinite sphere monodromy and rank-one Lie elements — original Lemma 4.10.**</p><p>Assume \\(n\\geq3\\) in Proposition 7. If \\(M_\\chi\\) is infinite, \\(M_\\chi^\\circ\\) acts irreducibly on \\(W_\\chi\\), and \\(\\operatorname{Lie}(M_\\chi^\\circ)\\) contains a rank-one nilpotent.</p>", "proof": "<p>Consecutive two-puncture arcs act irreducibly. Their common fixed equations make all \\(s_i=z(g_i)/(1-t_i)\\) equal, so subtracting a coboundary kills the cocycle. Consecutive image directions pair nontrivially by Proposition 5; hence their graph is connected and they span. Thus \\(M_\\chi\\) is irreducible.</p><p>The unipotent radical of \\(M_\\chi^\\circ\\) is normal in \\(M_\\chi\\); its nonzero fixed space is full-group invariant and therefore all of \\(W_\\chi\\). Thus \\(M_\\chi^\\circ\\) is reductive in this representation. Its isotypic summands are transitively permuted.</p><p>If its Lie algebra already has a rank-one nilpotent, on an isotypic summand \\(S\\otimes A\\) it acts as \\(N_S\\otimes1_A\\). Nonzero action forces \\(\\dim A=1\\), \\(\\dim S\\geq2\\). Transitivity gives this for all summands. A rank-one change from a scalar cannot permute two summands of dimension at least two, so every arc transformation preserves them. Arc irreducibility forces one summand, giving connected irreducibility.</p><p>There are two immediate sources of such a nilpotent. If some \\(t_it_j=1\\), that pair twist is a nontrivial rank-one unipotent. If all active pair products equal \\(-1\\), all \\(t_i\\) are the same primitive fourth root. Then \\(4\\mid r\\), and \\(r\\geq5\\) gives \\(r\\geq8\\). Enclose four active points in a disk and let \\(g\\) be their meridian product. Since \\(t(g)=1\\), its boundary twist has displacement\n\\[\nz_i\\longmapsto z_i+(1-t_i)z(g)\\quad(i\\text{ inside}),\n\\qquad\nz_j\\longmapsto z_j\\quad(j\\text{ outside}).\n\\]\nIts image is nonzero modulo coboundaries because active points remain outside. Its functional is nonzero by choosing inside \\(z(g)\\neq0\\) and satisfying the global relation with an outside coordinate. The displacement squares to zero.</p><p>It remains that no pair product is one and some pair product is \\(\\lambda\\neq\\pm1\\). All pair twists are semisimple pseudoreflections. If connected isotypic summands have dimension at least two, the same rank argument makes every twist preserve each, hence there is one isotypic summand \\(S\\otimes A\\). If \\(\\dim S=1\\), the connected group acts by scalars and finite determinant makes it trivial, contradicting infinitude. Thus \\(\\dim S&gt;1\\).</p><p>An operator normalizing this isotypic action has form \\(B\\otimes D\\): choose an intertwiner \\(B\\) for the induced automorphism on \\(S\\), after which Schur's lemma leaves \\(1\\otimes D\\). A semisimple pseudoreflection cannot have this form with both dimensions at least two. Both factors must be semisimple, and their eigenvalue products form a rectangular grid with one exceptional entry. A \\(2\\times2\\) subrectangle containing that entry contradicts equality of the products of diagonal pairs. Hence \\(\\dim A=1\\), proving connected irreducibility in this case.</p><p>The remaining reducible possibility has distinct one-dimensional connected isotypic summands. The invariant Hermitian form pairs each line nondegenerately with exactly one other: for characters \\(a,b\\) of the connected group, nonzero pairing requires \\(a\\overline b=1\\) on the finite-index dense monodromy subgroup. This gives a pairing involution. Each pair twist preserves it and permutes the lines by the identity or one transposition, since an \\(\\ell\\)-cycle contributes rank at least \\(\\ell-1\\) to displacement from a scalar. The twist-generated permutation group is transitive. If the involution had no fixed point, every commuting transposition could only exchange the members of one pair, preventing transitivity on at least three lines. Thus every line is self-paired by transitivity, and their signs are equal. The form is definite.</p><p>The decomposition and pseudoreflection properties are algebraic over \\(k\\), hence persist under all scalar translates. The same argument makes every translated form definite, contradicting infinitude by Lemma 7.1. This proves connected irreducibility.</p><p>Normalize a pair twist of ratio \\(\\lambda\\neq\\pm1\\) to\n\\[\nR=\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda,1,\\ldots,1)\n\\]\non \\(W_\\chi=L\\oplus U\\), \\(\\dim L=1\\). Conjugation preserves \\(\\mathfrak m=\\operatorname{Lie}(M_\\chi^\\circ)\\), with distinct eigenvalues \\(1,\\lambda,\\lambda^{-1}\\) on\n\\[\n\\operatorname{End}(L)\\oplus\\operatorname{End}(U),\n\\quad\n\\operatorname{Hom}(U,L),\n\\quad\n\\operatorname{Hom}(L,U).\n\\]\nPolynomial eigenspace projections preserve \\(\\mathfrak m\\). Connected irreducibility forces a nonzero off-diagonal component. Such an element has rank one and square zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Admissible links between standard character spaces — original Proposition 4.13 and Lemma 4.14.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Admissible links between standard character spaces — original Proposition 4.13 and Lemma 4.14.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a Hodge-generic fiber of the full marked abelian-cover variation, with Hodge group \\(J\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Consider non-toric admissible character spaces, each with one supporting simple ideal of \\(\\operatorname{Lie}(J^{\\mathrm{der}})_k\\), as in Propositions 6 and 7.</p><p class=\"li\">A polarized dual is the opposite paired space, identified with \\(W^*(-1)\\). It reverses Hodge-circle weights.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For a sphere base, suppose \\(W_\\chi,W_{\\chi'}\\) have the same supporting ideal. They have the same dimension \\(n\\) and equivalent standard derived representations up to duality.</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(n\\geq3\\), then \\(\\chi'=\\chi\\) or \\(\\chi^{-1}\\). The inverse character is the polarized dual. If the standard group is symplectic, the character has order two and the space is mixed at every scalar translate.</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(n=2\\), choose either \\(W_\\chi\\) or its polarized dual so that an equivalence with \\(W_{\\chi'}\\) respects the Hodge circle after every scalar conjugation.</p><p>For arbitrary base genus, two admissible irreducible spaces with equivalent representations of one supporting simple ideal, both mixed at every simultaneous scalar conjugate, are admissibly Hodge-isomorphic. A dual comparison uses the opposite paired space. A mixed standard symplectic space has an alternating self-pair tensor.</p>", "proof": "<p>The arbitrary-genus mixed statements follow from Lemma 8.2. For the sphere comparison, let \\(\\mathfrak f\\) be the shared supporting ideal. Standard special linear and symplectic representations determine their dimension and are preserved, up to duality, by algebra isomorphisms; \\(\\mathfrak{sl}_2=\\mathfrak{sp}_2\\) has the same standard representation. Invert a character when necessary to obtain an \\(\\mathfrak f\\)-linear isomorphism\n\\[\nF:W_\\chi\\to W_{\\chi'}.\n\\]\nIt need not yet be Hodge.</p><p>The entire \\(\\Gamma\\)-actions are projectively intertwined by \\(F\\). Proposition 3 says that \\(\\Gamma\\) normalizes \\(J\\). Preservation of each character space preserves its projection kernel and hence its unique complementary supporting ideal \\(\\mathfrak f\\). The two implementations of the induced ideal automorphism differ by a scalar by Schur's lemma.</p><p>A two-puncture twist is projectively trivial if either endpoint is inactive, since that puncture can be filled. It is projectively nontrivial if both are active: \\(r=n+2\\geq4\\), the rank-one direction is nonzero, and an outside active coordinate satisfies the global relation while leaving its functional nonzero. Thus the nontrivial-pair graph is exactly the complete graph on the active set. Projective equivalence identifies the two active sets. Write their multipliers as \\(t_i,t'_i\\).</p><p>For \\(n\\geq3\\), eigenvalue multiplicities distinguish the exceptional eigenline from the fixed hyperplane. Hence\n\\[\nt'_it'_j=t_it_j\\qquad(i\\neq j),\n\\]\nalso for ratio one, where the twist is nontrivial unipotent. Image directions are intrinsic.</p><p>Choose three active points in a disk. Cocycles supported on them and zero on their product form a two-dimensional subspace \\(U\\subset W_\\chi\\), injectively in the cocycle quotient because active points remain outside. Consecutive pair directions span \\(U\\), so \\(F\\) identifies it with the primed counterpart. The three-point boundary twist acts on \\(U\\) by \\(t_it_jt_\\ell\\) and on \\(W_\\chi/U\\) by one. Its displacement is supported inside and zero on the product. Since \\(n-2&gt;0\\), the quotient fixes the projective comparison scalar, including a possible unipotent extension. Thus\n\\[\nt'_it'_jt'_\\ell=t_it_jt_\\ell.\n\\]\nThe pair equalities make every \\(t'_i/t_i\\) a common sign, and the triple equality makes that sign one. Sphere meridians generate the deck group, proving equality of characters after the permitted inversion. The symplectic case has order two by Proposition 7; internal complex conjugation exchanges Hodge parts, both of dimension \\(n/2\\), at every translate.</p><p>For \\(n=2\\), there are four active points. A projective \\(2\\times2\\) eigenvalue ratio is defined up to inversion:\n\\[\nt'_it'_j\\in\\{t_it_j,(t_it_j)^{-1}\\}.\n\\]\nPut \\(A=t_1t_2\\), \\(B=t_1t_3\\), \\(D=t_1t_4\\). The Klein-four permutations give triples\n\\[\n(A,B,D),\\quad\n(A,B^{-1},D^{-1}),\\quad\n(A^{-1},B,D^{-1}),\\quad\n(A^{-1},B^{-1},D).\n\\]\nUniform inversion supplies the other four choices. Choose a Klein-four permutation \\(\\pi\\) and \\(\\epsilon\\in\\{\\pm1\\}\\) realizing the three pair-product choices involving index one. Since \\(t_1^2=ABD\\), recovery leaves a common sign:\n\\[\nt'_i=\\delta\\,t_{\\pi(i)}^\\epsilon,\\qquad\n\\delta\\in\\{\\pm1\\}.\n\\]\nThese choices persist at every scalar translate.</p><p>Mixedness agrees at each translate. Split the infinitesimal Hodge-circle action into the central scalar part and its component in the common ideal. On a standard two-dimensional module the ideal component is zero exactly for a pure space. The same component acts on the equivalent modules.</p><p>Permuting multipliers leaves signatures unchanged; uniform inversion reverses pure signatures by Lemma 8.1. For the additional sign \\(\\delta=-1\\), no \\(v_i\\) equals \\(1/2\\), since that would create an inactive multiplier. If \\(a\\) entries exceed \\(1/2\\), the sign-changed sum is\n\\[\nS'=S+2-a.\n\\]\nFor \\(S=1\\), \\(a\\in\\{0,1\\}\\), and agreement of purity excludes \\(S'=2\\), leaving \\(a=0,S'=3\\). For \\(S=3\\), similarly \\(a=4,S'=1\\). For \\(S=2\\), agreement of mixedness gives \\(a=2,S'=2\\). Thus the common sign reverses pure orientation wherever pure. The relative pure orientation is the fixed sign \\(\\delta\\epsilon\\) at every pure translate.</p><p>Because the standard \\(\\mathfrak{sl}_2\\)-module is self-dual, choose the original or polarized-dual model according to this sign and an ideal intertwiner over \\(k\\). At a mixed translate, both circle spectra are \\(+1,-1\\), so the central scalar shift is zero; the intertwiner respects the common ideal and hence the circle. At a pure translate, the circles are the same scalar. The argument applies to every conjugate of the chosen intertwiner. This proves simultaneous admissibility without assuming commutation of arbitrary scalar automorphisms with complex conjugation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Four-point signatures — original Lemma 4.12.** Suppose a sphere character has four active multipliers \\( t_i=\\exp(2\\pi i v_i),\\qquad0<v_i<1, \\qquad S=\\sum_{i=1}^4v_i\\in\\{1,2,3\\}. \\) In a fixed character convention …", "statement": "<p>**Four-point signatures — original Lemma 4.12.**</p><p>Suppose a sphere character has four active multipliers\n\\[\nt_i=\\exp(2\\pi i v_i),\\qquad0&lt;v_i&lt;1,\n\\qquad S=\\sum_{i=1}^4v_i\\in\\{1,2,3\\}.\n\\]\nIn a fixed character convention, its Hodge multiplicities are \\(S-1\\) and \\(3-S\\). Thus \\(S=2\\) means mixed, while \\(S=1,3\\) are opposite pure types.</p>", "proof": "<p>Pass to the faithful cyclic quotient of order \\(m\\), and write \\(v_i=a_i/m\\), \\(0&lt;a_i&lt;m\\). Choose infinity away from the branch points. The equation is\n\\[\ny^m=\\prod_i(x-b_i)^{a_i},\n\\qquad \\sum_i a_i=mS.\n\\]\nEigendifferentials inverse to the character of \\(y\\) are \\(f(x)\\,dx/y\\). At \\(b_i\\), with \\(d_i=\\gcd(m,a_i)\\), a uniformizer has\n\\[\nx-b_i=u^{m/d_i},\n\\qquad\ny=(\\text{unit})u^{a_i/d_i}.\n\\]\nThe differential \\(dx/y\\) is holomorphic, while a pole of \\(f\\) would destroy holomorphicity, so \\(f\\) is a polynomial. At infinity, \\(\\deg f\\leq S-2\\), giving dimension \\(S-1\\). Replacing \\(y\\) by \\(\\prod_i(x-b_i)/y\\) gives exponents \\(m-a_i\\) and dimension \\(3-S\\). Reversing the character convention exchanges the two dimensions throughout.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Links between mixed standard spaces — original Lemma 4.14.** Let two admissible irreducible spaces in the same polarized weight-one structure have equivalent representations of one supporting simple ideal of the …", "statement": "<p>**Links between mixed standard spaces — original Lemma 4.14.**</p><p>Let two admissible irreducible spaces in the same polarized weight-one structure have equivalent representations of one supporting simple ideal of the derived Hodge Lie algebra. Assume both are mixed at every simultaneous scalar conjugate. Then an intertwiner is an admissible Hodge isomorphism. For a dual comparison, replace one space by its opposite paired space.</p><p>If a space satisfying these mixedness assumptions has standard symplectic derived action, its invariant alternating tensor is a self-pair tensor.</p>", "proof": "<p>Schur's lemma makes the intertwiner line one-dimensional. It is Hodge-group stable because the derived group is normal. If the Hodge circle acts on it by \\(z^a\\), the intertwiner shifts one spectrum by \\(a\\). Both spectra are \\(\\{-1,1\\}\\), forcing \\(a=0\\). Repeat after every scalar conjugation. The map is admissible and is algebraic by the cited admissible-operations result.</p><p>The invariant nondegenerate alternating form also spans a Hodge-stable line. Its scalar weight is zero: nondegeneracy pairs the spectrum \\(\\{-1,1\\}\\) with its reflection about half that weight, whose midpoint must be zero. Its inverse tensor has type \\((1,1)\\), as do all scalar conjugates.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Saturated data for the marked cover family — original Proposition 4.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Saturated data for the marked cover family — original Proposition 4.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(s\\in\\mathcal T_\\varphi^{\\mathrm{Hg}}\\), \\(A=\\operatorname{Jac}(C_s)\\),\n  \\[\n  H=H^1(A,\\mathbb Q),\\quad J=\\operatorname{Hdg}(H),\\quad R=J^{\\mathrm{der}}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The cited saturated determinant criterion, original Companion results 2.2, calls the following data saturated:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. A rational Hodge summand \\(H_0\\subset H\\) with toric Hodge group, with \\(H_{0,k}\\) decomposed into CM embedding lines, distinguishing repeated occurrences.</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. An admissible block decomposition of the complement of \\(H_{0,k}\\), with actual representatives \\(V_i\\) among the summands. Symplectic or orthogonal blocks use alternating or symmetric self-pairs; linear blocks, with \\(\\dim V_i\\geq2\\), use copies of \\(V_i\\) and \\(\\overline V_i\\), paired with each other.</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">3. Containment in the image of \\(R_k\\) of\n     \\[\n     \\prod_{i\\in I_{\\mathrm{Sp}}}\\operatorname{Sp}(V_i)\n     \\times\n     \\prod_{i\\in I_{\\mathrm{SO}}}\\operatorname{SO}(V_i)\n     \\times\n     \\prod_{i\\in I_{\\mathrm{SL}}}\\operatorname{SL}(V_i),\n     \\]\n     acting standardly on the corresponding copies, dually on opposite linear copies, and trivially elsewhere.</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">4. CM supply for each singleton \\(D(V_i)\\) in an orthogonal or linear block, without a balance condition.</p><p class=\"li\">The forms are inverse to the specified pair tensors. In a linear block,\n  \\(\\overline V_i\\simeq V_i^*(-1)\\) as Hodge-graded spaces.</p><p class=\"li\">The cited criterion asserts that saturated data make every rational balanced tensor algebraic on separate \\(A\\)-slots and imply the rational Hodge conjecture on every self-power. The data remain saturated after adjoining any CM abelian variety.</p><p class=\"li\">The cited toric-summand input, original Companion results 2.1(iv), realizes a rational toric degree-one Hodge summand as \\(H^1\\) of a CM isogeny factor.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The Jacobian \\(A\\) admits saturated data. No orthogonal blocks are required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose an Abel map \\(a:C_s\\to A\\). Its pullback on first cohomology is a rational Hodge isomorphism; its inverse is an algebraic degree-one operation by the admissible-operations input. Transport the character projectors\n\\[\ne_\\chi=\\frac1{|P|}\\sum_{g\\in P}\\chi(g)^{-1}g\n\\]\nto actual summands of \\(H^1(A,k)\\). They and all scalar conjugates preserve the Hodge decomposition.</p><p>Collect rational Galois orbits of character spaces on which \\(J^{\\mathrm{der}}\\) is trivial into \\(H_0\\). Its Hodge group is a torus, since its projections are tori and its group embeds in their product. The toric-summand input realizes it as a CM isogeny factor and gives the required embedding-line decomposition.</p><p>All remaining nonzero character spaces have standard special linear or symplectic derived projections, by Proposition 6 for positive base genus and Proposition 7 for the sphere. Each is supported by one simple ideal, mapping isomorphically onto its simple image. Two such spaces with the same ideal have equivalent or dual standard representations; the only coincidence between the types is \\(\\mathfrak{sl}_2=\\mathfrak{sp}_2\\).</p><p>Proposition 8 makes these comparisons admissible Hodge isomorphisms, using opposite characters for dual comparisons. This includes the simultaneous alignment of pure scalar translates in the sphere dimension-two case. Group spaces with the same ideal into a block. Treat two-dimensional blocks as linear. Higher-dimensional symplectic blocks have alternating self-pairs, from the mixed-space statement in positive genus and from the restricted polarization on order-two sphere characters. Linear blocks use polarization between a character and its opposite, which is its coefficient-conjugate space because character values are roots of unity.</p><p>Simple ideals give simultaneous independent standard actions: each acts precisely on its own block and trivially on other blocks and on \\(H_0\\). The connected image of \\(J_k^{\\mathrm{der}}\\) has the Lie algebra of the product of the asserted standard groups, hence the same connected group image. This gives the product containment. No independence of the central torus is needed.</p><p>Each representative is the transported image of an actual character space. Proposition 2 supplies its determinant, using a faithful cyclic quotient for a nontrivial character and polarization for the invariant character. Curve–Jacobian maps and admissible block identifications transfer this supply to the chosen Jacobian slots. Every linear singleton is CM-supplied; there are no orthogonal blocks. The data are saturated.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Coordinate-root geometry and exact smooth locus — original Definition 5.1 and Lemma 5.4, with Proposition 5.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Coordinate-root geometry and exact smooth locus — original Definition 5.1 and Lemma 5.4, with Proposition 5.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(m,n\\geq2\\), \\(0\\leq d\\leq m-2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A \\((d+1)\\)-dimensional subspace \\(P\\subset\\mathbb C^m\\) is coordinate-general if every \\(d+1\\) coordinate functionals restrict to a basis of \\(P^*\\). Write \\(\\mathcal U_{d,m}\\subset\\operatorname{Gr}(d+1,m)\\) for this locus.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  X_P=\\{[x_i]\\in\\mathbb P^{m-1}:(x_1^n,\\ldots,x_m^n)\\in P\\},\n  \\quad\n  G_{m,n}=(\\mu_n)^m/\\mu_n,\n  \\]\n  with diagonal denominator, and\n  \\[\n  q_P:X_P\\to\\mathbb P(P),\\qquad[x_i]\\mapsto[x_i^n].\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(P\\in\\mathcal U_{d,m}\\), \\(X_P\\) is a smooth projective complete intersection of dimension \\(d\\), connected if \\(d\\geq1\\). The map \\(q_P\\) is finite and is the \\(G_{m,n}\\)-quotient. Coordinate hyperplanes on \\(\\mathbb P(P)\\), and their reduced inverse images, have simple normal crossings. For \\(d=0\\), \\(X_P\\) is a reduced \\(G_{m,n}\\)-torsor.</p><p>The locus \\(\\mathcal U_{d,m}\\) is nonempty and connected, and these varieties form a smooth projective family with phase action.</p><p>For a rank-\\(c\\) matrix \\(A\\), \\(1\\leq c\\leq m-1\\), the smooth locus of \\(X_A\\) is exactly the nonvanishing locus of every \\(c\\)-column minor; this is coordinate generality of \\(\\ker A\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Any at most \\(d+1\\) coordinate restrictions on \\(P\\) are independent. At most \\(d\\) coordinates vanish at a point of \\(\\mathbb P(P)\\). If \\(a\\) vanish, their ratios to a nonvanishing coordinate have independent differentials and extend to relative coordinates \\(t_1,\\ldots,t_d\\). After an étale base change taking roots of remaining units, the cover is\n\\[\nz_1^n=t_1,\\ldots,z_a^n=t_a,\n\\]\nwith free \\(t_{a+1},\\ldots,t_d\\). It is smooth, and its boundary is \\(z_1\\cdots z_a=0\\). The calculation applies to the universal projective bundle, proving relative smoothness and normal crossings. Lemma 10.1 gives the complete-intersection description.</p><p>Fibers of \\(q_P\\) are exactly phase orbits. For \\(d=0\\), no base coordinate vanishes, so the fiber is a reduced free transitive phase set. For \\(d\\geq1\\), Lemma 10.2 gives connectedness and ambient cohomology away from the middle.</p><p>For distinct \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_m\\), the evaluation space\n\\[\n\\{(f(a_1),\\ldots,f(a_m)):\\deg f\\leq d\\}\n\\]\nhas nonzero maximal minors by Vandermonde. Thus \\(\\mathcal U_{d,m}\\) is a nonempty open of an irreducible Grassmannian, hence connected as a complex manifold. Universal incidence equations are projective, and the local calculation proves smoothness compatibly with phases.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Exact smooth locus — original Proposition 5.2.** Let \\(n\\geq2\\), \\(1\\leq c\\leq m-1\\), and let \\(A\\) be a rank-\\(c\\) matrix with \\(m\\) columns.", "statement": "<p>**Exact smooth locus — original Proposition 5.2.**</p><p>Let \\(n\\geq2\\), \\(1\\leq c\\leq m-1\\), and let \\(A\\) be a rank-\\(c\\) matrix with \\(m\\) columns. Then \\(X_A\\) is a complete intersection of dimension \\(m-c-1\\). It is smooth if and only if every \\(c\\)-column minor of \\(A\\) is nonzero, equivalently if \\(P=\\ker A\\) is coordinate-general.</p>", "proof": "<p>The ring map\n\\[\n\\mathbb C[t_1,\\ldots,t_m]\\to\\mathbb C[x_1,\\ldots,x_m],\n\\qquad t_i\\mapsto x_i^n,\n\\]\nis free on monomials \\(\\prod_i x_i^{e_i}\\), \\(0\\leq e_i&lt;n\\). Flatness preserves the regular sequence of independent linear equations defining \\(\\ker A\\). The projective coordinate-power map is finite surjective, so its inverse image has dimension \\(m-c-1\\).</p><p>At a point with \\(I=\\{i:x_i\\neq0\\}\\), the Jacobian has columns \\(na_i x_i^{n-1}\\) for \\(i\\in I\\), zero otherwise. If every \\(c\\)-minor is nonzero, every set of at most \\(c\\) columns is independent. The equation\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\in I}a_i x_i^n=0\n\\]\nforces \\(|I|&gt;c\\), and the Jacobian has rank \\(c\\).</p><p>Conversely, a vanishing \\(c\\)-minor gives a nonzero dependence on those columns. Take \\(n\\)th roots of the nonzero coefficients and set the remaining coordinates to zero. The resulting point has Jacobian rank below \\(c\\); here \\(n\\geq2\\) makes zero coordinates yield zero Jacobian columns.</p><p>For \\(|S|=m-c\\), projection \\(\\ker A\\to\\mathbb C^S\\) is an isomorphism precisely when no nonzero kernel vector is supported on the complementary \\(c\\) coordinates, equivalently when that complementary minor is nonzero.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Cohomology outside the middle degree — original Lemma 5.3.** Let \\(X_A\\) be a smooth diagonal complete intersection of dimension \\(d\\geq1\\), and put \\(h=c_1(\\mathcal O_{X_A}(1))\\).", "statement": "<p>**Cohomology outside the middle degree — original Lemma 5.3.**</p><p>Let \\(X_A\\) be a smooth diagonal complete intersection of dimension \\(d\\geq1\\), and put \\(h=c_1(\\mathcal O_{X_A}(1))\\). For \\(0\\leq q\\leq2d\\), \\(q\\neq d\\),\n\\[\nH^q(X_A,\\mathbb Q)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\mathbb Q h^{q/2},&amp;q\\text{ even},\\\\\n0,&amp;q\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nIn particular \\(X_A\\) is connected.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(Q\\) be the row space of \\(A\\). For every \\(j\\leq c\\) and \\(j\\)-element coordinate set \\(I\\), the projection \\(Q\\to\\mathbb C^I\\) is surjective: otherwise a dependent set of \\(j\\) columns could be extended to a dependent set of \\(c\\) columns.</p><p>In the complete flag variety of \\(Q\\), requiring the \\(j\\)-plane to project isomorphically to \\(\\mathbb C^I\\) is nonempty open, by choosing a complement to the projection kernel and extending it to a flag. Irreducibility gives a flag\n\\[\n0=Q_0\\subset Q_1\\subset\\cdots\\subset Q_c=Q\n\\]\nsatisfying all finitely many conditions. Its diagonal zero loci \\(Y_j\\), with \\(Y_0=\\mathbb P^{m-1}\\), are smooth by Lemma 10.1. Under the degree-\\(n\\) Veronese embedding, each next equation is a hyperplane section.</p><p>Successive applications of the Lefschetz hyperplane theorem [15, Corollary 7.3] give\n\\[\nH_i(X_A,\\mathbb Z)\\xrightarrow{\\sim}H_i(\\mathbb P^{m-1},\\mathbb Z)\n\\qquad(i&lt;d).\n\\]\nUniversal coefficients give the corresponding rational cohomology restriction isomorphisms and connectedness.</p><p>Poincaré duality gives dimensions above \\(d\\). The ambient powers are nonzero because\n\\[\n\\int_{X_A}h^d=n^c&gt;0;\n\\]\nvanishing of any \\(h^r\\), \\(0\\leq r\\leq d\\), would force \\(h^d=0\\). They span the one-dimensional even groups.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Character cohomology and support — original Proposition 5.6.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Character cohomology and support — original Proposition 5.6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P\\in\\mathcal U_{d,m}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Identify phase characters with\n  \\[\n  b=(b_1,\\ldots,b_m)\\in(\\mathbb Z/n)^m,\n  \\qquad \\sum_i b_i=0,\n  \\qquad\n  \\chi_b([\\zeta_i])=\\prod_i\\zeta_i^{b_i}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Subscripts \\(b\\) denote pullback eigenspaces. Put\n  \\[\n  S(b)=\\{i:b_i\\neq0\\},\\quad s(b)=|S(b)|,\\quad\n  W_{P,b}=H^d(X_P,\\overline{\\mathbb Q})_b.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Invariant cohomology is the pullback of \\(H^*(\\mathbb P(P),\\mathbb Q)\\), spanned by algebraic hyperplane classes. Every nontrivial character occurs only in degree \\(d\\), and\n\\[\n\\dim_{\\overline{\\mathbb Q}}W_{P,b}\n=\n\\binom{s(b)-2}{d}\\qquad(b\\neq0),\n\\]\nwith value zero if \\(s(b)-2&lt;d\\).</p><p>If \\(S=S(b)\\) has \\(s\\geq d+2\\), projection identifies \\(P\\) with a coordinate-general subspace \\(P_S\\subset\\mathbb C^S\\), and defines a finite quotient\n\\[\np_S:X_P\\to X_{P_S}\n\\]\nwhose pullback identifies the \\(b|_S\\)-space with \\(W_{P,b}\\). These descriptions commute with smooth parameter pullback.</p>", "proof": "<p>Finite quotient cohomology gives the invariant assertion. A base hyperplane pulls back to \\(n\\) times a root hyperplane. For \\(d\\geq1\\), Proposition 10 makes every nonmiddle group ambient and phase-invariant. For \\(d=0\\), degree zero is the regular representation of the finite torsor.</p><p>For \\(d\\geq1\\), compute at an evaluation plane. The curve \\(C\\to\\mathbb P^1\\) has branch points at the zeros of its coordinate forms. In the effective cyclic quotient for \\(b\\), exactly \\(s=s(b)\\) are active. Its rank-one character sheaf is extension by zero there, with compactly supported Euler characteristic \\(2-s\\). Degrees zero and two have no nontrivial character, so\n\\[\n\\dim V_b=s-2.\n\\]\nThis also handles nonfaithful characters and nonunit entries. Lemma 11.1 gives the binomial rank. Character projectors cut out local systems on connected \\(\\mathcal U_{d,m}\\), so ranks are constant. For \\(d=0\\), every character has rank one; a nonzero sum-zero tuple has \\(s\\geq2\\), agreeing with the formula.</p><p>For \\(s\\geq d+2\\), any \\(d+1\\) chosen coordinates detect \\(P\\). Its projection is injective with coordinate-general image, and the selected root coordinates cannot all vanish. Forgetting others defines \\(p_S\\). Its pullback of \\(\\mathcal O(1)\\) is \\(\\mathcal O_{X_P}(1)\\), proving finiteness by the ample-bundle argument. Taking roots of the remaining determined base coordinates proves surjectivity.</p><p>Fibers are precisely orbits of\n\\[\nK_S=\\ker(G_{m,n}\\to G_{s,n}).\n\\]\nFor \\(d\\geq1\\), the finite map \\(X_P/K_S\\to X_{P_S}\\) is birational with smooth target, hence an isomorphism. For \\(d=0\\), it is directly the quotient of finite torsors. Since \\(b\\) inflates from \\(G_{s,n}\\), Lemma 1.1 gives the eigenspace isomorphism. Universal coordinate projections and constant phase actions give family compatibility.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Evaluation quotient — original Lemma 5.5.** Choose distinct \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_m\\in\\mathbb C\\), \\(m\\geq3\\), and set \\( P_j=\\{(f(a_1),\\ldots,f(a_m)):\\deg f\\leq j\\}, \\quad1\\leq j\\leq m-2, \\qquad C=X_{P_1}. \\) Coordinate …", "statement": "<p>**Evaluation quotient — original Lemma 5.5.**</p><p>Choose distinct \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_m\\in\\mathbb C\\), \\(m\\geq3\\), and set\n\\[\nP_j=\\{(f(a_1),\\ldots,f(a_m)):\\deg f\\leq j\\},\n\\quad1\\leq j\\leq m-2,\n\\qquad C=X_{P_1}.\n\\]\nCoordinate multiplication is a finite surjective morphism\n\\[\n\\mu_j:C^j\\to X_{P_j},\n\\qquad\n(x^{(1)},\\ldots,x^{(j)})\n\\longmapsto\n\\left[\\prod_{\\nu=1}^j x_i^{(\\nu)}\\right]_{i=1}^m,\n\\]\nidentifying the target with\n\\[\nC^j\\Big/\n\\left(\\ker(G_{m,n}^j\\xrightarrow{\\prod}G_{m,n})\\rtimes\\mathfrak S_j\\right).\n\\]\nFor \\(b\\neq0\\), pullback identifies\n\\[\nH^j(X_{P_j},\\overline{\\mathbb Q})_b\n\\simeq\n\\bigwedge^jV_b,\n\\qquad V_b=H^1(C,\\overline{\\mathbb Q})_b,\n\\]\nembedded by antisymmetrization.</p>", "proof": "<p>Every curve point has at most one zero coordinate. Since \\(j&lt;m\\), the products are not all zero. Products of evaluations of linear polynomials evaluate their product, so the formula gives a morphism. Its defining sections give\n\\[\n\\mu_j^*\\mathcal O(1)=\\mathcal O_C(1)\\boxtimes\\cdots\\boxtimes\\mathcal O_C(1),\n\\]\nwhich is ample. A positive-dimensional projective fiber would contain a curve of degree zero for this bundle, impossible. Thus the map is finite.</p><p>Homogenization identifies polynomials of degree at most \\(j\\) with binary degree-\\(j\\) forms. Every such form factors into linear forms, including a factor for infinity. Choose roots of their evaluation vectors; changing the phase on one factor realizes any prescribed root vector over the product. This gives surjectivity. Generically the only identifications are factor permutations and phase tuples of total product one. The indicated quotient therefore maps finitely and birationally to the smooth target, hence isomorphically by normality.</p><p>By Lemma 1.1, target cohomology is quotient-invariant product cohomology. In residual character \\(b\\), product-kernel invariance requires character \\(b\\) in every factor. Connectedness excludes nontrivial characters in degrees zero and two, leaving \\(V_b^{\\otimes j}\\). A geometric interchange of degree-one factors is minus ordinary interchange, so permutation invariants are alternating tensors.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Perfect Gale tensor — original Theorem 6.1.** Let \\(P\\in\\mathcal U_{d,m}\\), put \\( Q=P^\\perp \\quad\\text{for the ordinary bilinear form }\\sum_i u_iv_i, \\qquad d'=m-d-2. \\) Then \\(Q\\in\\mathcal U_{d',m}\\), and coordinate …", "statement": "<p>**Perfect Gale tensor — original Theorem 6.1.**</p><p>Let \\(P\\in\\mathcal U_{d,m}\\), put\n\\[\nQ=P^\\perp\n\\quad\\text{for the ordinary bilinear form }\\sum_i u_iv_i,\n\\qquad\nd'=m-d-2.\n\\]\nThen \\(Q\\in\\mathcal U_{d',m}\\), and coordinate multiplication defines an algebraic morphism\n\\[\nf_P:X_P\\times X_Q\\to F_n^{m-2},\n\\qquad([x_i],[y_i])\\mapsto[x_iy_i].\n\\]</p><p>For each full-support character \\(b\\),\n\\[\nL_b=H^{m-2}(F_n^{m-2},\\overline{\\mathbb Q})_b\n\\]\nis a line, and pullback maps it to \\(W_{P,b}\\otimes W_{Q,b}\\). For every \\(0\\neq\\ell\\in L_b\\), \\(f_P^*\\ell\\) has full pairing rank:\n\\[\nW_{P,b}^*\\xrightarrow{\\sim}W_{Q,b}.\n\\]\nThis includes \\(d=0\\) and \\(d'=0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Maximal minors of the bilinear orthogonal complement are, up to common scalar and signs, complementary maximal minors of \\(P\\), by the standard volume pairing on top exterior powers. Thus \\(Q\\) is coordinate-general.</p><p>Points of \\(X_P,X_Q\\) have at most \\(d,d'\\) zero coordinates. Their product has at most \\(m-2\\) zeros, so defines a morphism. Its image is Fermat because\n\\[\n\\sum_i(x_iy_i)^n=\\sum_i x_i^ny_i^n=0.\n\\]\nEquivariance for phase multiplication sends \\(b\\) to \\((b,b)\\). Proposition 11 leaves only middle degrees and, on the Fermat hyperplane, gives \\(\\dim L_b=1\\).</p><p>Assume first \\(d,d'&gt;0\\), and put \\(N=m-2=d+d'\\). At evaluation planes \\(P_j\\), let\n\\[\nc_i=\\prod_{h\\neq i}(a_i-a_h)^{-1}.\n\\]\nLagrange interpolation, comparing the \\(z^{m-1}\\) coefficient for \\(z^e\\), gives\n\\[\n\\sum_i c_i a_i^e=0\\qquad(0\\leq e\\leq m-2).\n\\]\nProducts of degrees at most \\(d,d'\\) have degree at most \\(m-2\\). Dimension and these identities give\n\\[\nP_d^\\perp=\\operatorname{diag}(c_i)P_{d'},\n\\qquad\n\\{(z_i):\\sum_i z_i=0\\}=\\operatorname{diag}(c_i)P_N.\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\xi_i^n=c_i\\). Coordinate scaling \\(s_\\xi([z_i])=[\\xi_i z_i]\\) gives phase-equivariant isomorphisms\n\\[\nX_{P_{d'}}\\simeq X_{P_d^\\perp},\n\\qquad\nX_{P_N}\\simeq F_n^N.\n\\]\nThe actual multiplication maps satisfy\n\\[\nf_{P_d}\\circ\\bigl(\\mu_d\\times(s_\\xi\\circ\\mu_{d'})\\bigr)\n=\ns_\\xi\\circ\\mu_N\n\\]\non \\(C^N=C^d\\times C^{d'}\\): both sides give\n\\[\n\\left[\\xi_i\\prod_{\\nu=1}^N x_i^{(\\nu)}\\right].\n\\]</p><p>For \\(V=H^1(C,\\overline{\\mathbb Q})_b\\), Proposition 11 and its evaluation quotient identify the three spaces with\n\\[\n\\bigwedge^NV,\\qquad\\bigwedge^dV,\\qquad\\bigwedge^{d'}V,\n\\]\nand full support gives \\(\\dim V=N\\). Use the unnormalized alternating inclusion\n\\[\n\\iota_j(v_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge v_j)\n=\n\\sum_{\\sigma\\in\\mathfrak S_j}\n\\operatorname{sgn}(\\sigma)\nv_{\\sigma(1)}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes v_{\\sigma(j)}.\n\\]\nEquality of the multiplication maps makes pullback the exterior coproduct, whose composite with \\(\\iota_d\\otimes\\iota_{d'}\\) is \\(\\iota_N\\). For a basis,\n\\[\nv_1\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge v_N\n\\longmapsto\n\\sum_{\\substack{I\\subset\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}\\\\|I|=d}}\n\\operatorname{sgn}(I,I^c)\\,v_I\\otimes v_{I^c}.\n\\]\nComplementary wedge bases are paired bijectively with nonzero coefficients, giving rank \\(\\binom Nd\\).</p><p>The map \\(P\\mapsto P^\\perp\\) is algebraic; bilinear, not Hermitian, orthogonality matters here. The \\(f_P\\) form a morphism from the smooth projective product family to a fixed Fermat variety. Cohomological pullback is a local-system morphism; in flat character frames the tensor and its rank are constant. Connectedness of \\(\\mathcal U_{d,m}\\) extends full rank to all parameters.</p><p>If \\(d=0\\), choose a root point \\(x\\) over \\(\\mathbb P(P)\\). All \\(x_i\\neq0\\), and multiplication by \\(x\\) identifies \\(X_Q\\) with \\(F_n^{m-2}\\). Thus restriction to \\(\\{x\\}\\times X_Q\\) is an isomorphism and has nonzero pullback on \\(L_b\\). A nonzero eigenfunction on a finite torsor is nonzero everywhere, so evaluation detects a nonzero \\((b,b)\\)-tensor. Both character ranks are one. The case \\(d'=0\\) is symmetric, including \\(m=2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Gale cohomology supply and low-codimension sources — original Proposition 6.2 and Lemma 6.3.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Gale cohomology supply and low-codimension sources — original Proposition 6.2 and Lemma 6.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P\\in\\mathcal U_{d,m}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(S\\subset\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}\\), \\(s=|S|\\geq d+2\\), put\n  \\[\n  P_S=\\operatorname{pr}_SP,\\quad Q_S=P_S^\\perp,\\quad\n  Y_S=X_{Q_S},\\quad h_S=s-d-2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A correspondence\n  \\[\n  \\Gamma\\in\\mathrm{CH}^{\\dim T+j}(T\\times X)_{\\mathbb Q}\n  \\]\n  acts by\n  \\[\n  \\Gamma_*\\alpha=(\\operatorname{pr}_X)_*\n  (\\operatorname{pr}_T^*\\alpha\\smile[\\Gamma]),\n  \\]\n  giving \\(H^{q-2j}(T,\\mathbb Q)(-j)\\to H^q(X,\\mathbb Q)\\). Morphism pullback is represented by the transpose graph.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a rational algebraic correspondence from \\(F_n^{s-2}\\times Y_S\\) to \\(X_P\\), of degree shift \\(-2h_S\\), whose scalar-extended image contains every \\(W_{P,b}\\) with support \\(S\\). In the middle degree it acts on the entire group\n\\[\nH^{d+2h_S}(F_n^{s-2}\\times Y_S,\\mathbb Q)(h_S).\n\\]\nTogether with hyperplane cycles from a point, these finitely many maps span the full rational cohomology of \\(X_P\\).</p><p>When \\(c=m-d-1\\in\\{1,2\\}\\), the contributing sources have precisely the forms specified in Lemma 13.1.</p>", "proof": "<p>First target \\(X=X_{P_S}\\), and write \\(h=h_S\\), \\(F=F_n^{s-2}\\), \\(Y=Y_S\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\dim F=d+h,\\qquad \\dim X=d,\\qquad \\dim Y=h.\n\\]\nThe graph embedding\n\\[\nX\\times Y\\to(F\\times Y)\\times X,\n\\qquad\n(x,y)\\mapsto(f_{P_S}(x,y),y,x)\n\\]\ndefines\n\\[\n\\Gamma_S\\in\\mathrm{CH}^{d+h}(F\\times Y\\times X)_{\\mathbb Q}.\n\\]\nThe source dimension is \\(d+2h\\), so its shift is \\(-2h\\). On Künneth tensors,\n\\[\n\\alpha\\otimes\\beta\\longmapsto\n(\\operatorname{pr}_X)_*\n\\bigl(f_{P_S}^*\\alpha\\smile\\operatorname{pr}_Y^*\\beta\\bigr).\n\\]</p><p>For \\(b\\) supported on \\(S\\), write \\(b_S=b|_S\\). Invariant Poincaré pairing on \\(Y\\) pairs exactly opposite characters and is perfect on each pair, including the sum pairing on a finite torsor. It identifies\n\\[\nH^h(Y,\\overline{\\mathbb Q})_{-b_S}(h)\n\\]\nwith the dual of \\(H^h(Y,\\overline{\\mathbb Q})_{b_S}\\). Proposition 12 therefore makes the graph action an isomorphism\n\\[\nL_{b_S}\\otimes H^h(Y,\\overline{\\mathbb Q})_{-b_S}(h)\n\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\nH^d(X,\\overline{\\mathbb Q})_{b_S}.\n\\]\nCompose with \\(p_S^*\\) from Proposition 11 to fill \\(W_{P,b}\\).</p><p>Both graphs are rational. Apply them to whole source degrees without character projectors. After scalar extension their images contain every indicated character piece. Hyperplane cycles supply invariant cohomology, including all nonmiddle groups. The resulting finite rational sum is surjective after extension to \\(\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\), so its rational cokernel is zero. No algebraic Künneth projector is required.</p><p>Lemma 13.1 gives the low-codimension source description. Each source retains its Fermat factor; the only possible contributing positive-dimensional dual factor is the one full-support curve in codimension two.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.1", "orig": "in codimension at most two — original Lemma 6.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(c=m-d-1\\in\\{1,2\\}\\), and let \\(b\\neq0\\) satisfy \\(W_{P,b}\\neq0\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(c=m-d-1\\in\\{1,2\\}\\), and let \\(b\\neq0\\) satisfy \\(W_{P,b}\\neq0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(c=1\\), \\(b\\) has full support and \\(Y_{S(b)}\\) is finite.</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(c=2\\), the support has size \\(m\\) or \\(m-1\\). Full support gives the connected curve\n  \\[\n  C=X_{P^\\perp}.\n  \\]\n  For \\(S=\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}\\setminus\\{i\\}\\), \\(Y_S\\) is the reduced boundary fiber \\(\\{y_i=0\\}\\) of \\(C\\), with that coordinate omitted, and consists of \\(n^{m-2}\\) points.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 11 gives \\(s\\geq d+2\\), and\n\\[\n\\dim Y_S=s-d-2.\n\\]\nFor \\(c=1\\), \\(m=d+2\\), so only \\(s=m\\) occurs and the dimension is zero. For \\(c=2\\), \\(m=d+3\\), so \\(s=m,m-1\\) give dimensions one and zero. Proposition 10 makes the full-support curve connected.</p><p>Zero extension identifies\n\\[\n(\\operatorname{pr}_SP)^\\perp\n=\n\\{q|_S:q\\in P^\\perp,\\ q_i=0\\}.\n\\]\nThis is the coordinate-general line cut from the dual two-plane by its \\(i\\)th coordinate hyperplane. Its root torsor is exactly the indicated reduced fiber; the phase group has order \\(n^{m-2}\\). Different boundary fibers are disjoint because a root-curve point has at most one zero coordinate.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**The branch map and coefficient product — original Proposition 7.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**The branch map and coefficient product — original Proposition 7.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(m\\geq3\\), \\(Q\\in\\mathcal D_{2,m}\\), and choose a row frame \\(A=(a_1,\\ldots,a_m)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\Delta_{ij}=\\det(a_i,a_j)\\). The \\(i\\)th coordinate form on \\(\\mathbb P(Q)\\) is\n  \\[\n  \\ell_i(s,t)=a_{1i}s+a_{2i}t,\n  \\]\n  with zero \\(p_i=[-a_{2i}:a_{1i}]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  M_m=\n  \\{(z_4,\\ldots,z_m)\\in(\\mathbb A^1\\setminus\\{0,1\\})^{m-3}:\n  z_i\\neq z_j\\},\n  \\]\n  with \\(M_3\\) a point. Normalizing the first three branch labels to\n  \\(\\infty=[1:0]\\), \\(0=[0:1]\\), \\(1=[1:1]\\) identifies \\(M_m\\simeq M_{0,m}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The normalized branch map is regular:\n\\[\n\\lambda:\\mathcal D_{2,m}\\to M_m,\n\\qquad\nz_i=\\frac{\\Delta_{2i}\\Delta_{13}}{\\Delta_{1i}\\Delta_{23}}\n\\quad(i\\geq4).\n\\]\nThere is a global product isomorphism\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_{2,m}\\simeq M_m\\times(\\mathbb G_m)^{m-1}\n\\]\nunder which \\(\\lambda\\) is projection. It is smooth surjective, and its quotient by the effective coefficient torus \\((\\mathbb G_m)^m/\\mathbb G_m\\) is \\(M_m\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The ratios are row-basis independent and have unit denominators. Plücker relations give\n\\[\nz_i-1=\\frac{\\Delta_{12}\\Delta_{3i}}{\\Delta_{1i}\\Delta_{23}},\n\\qquad\nz_i-z_j=\n-\\frac{\\Delta_{12}\\Delta_{13}\\Delta_{ij}}\n{\\Delta_{23}\\Delta_{1i}\\Delta_{1j}},\n\\]\nso values lie in \\(M_m\\).</p><p>Let \\(A_0(z)\\) have columns\n\\[\n(0,1)^t,\\ (1,0)^t,\\ (1,-1)^t,\\\n(1,-z_4)^t,\\ldots,(1,-z_m)^t.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nt_1=1,\\qquad\nt_2=\\frac{\\Delta_{23}}{\\Delta_{13}},\n\\quad\nt_3=\\frac{\\Delta_{23}}{\\Delta_{12}},\n\\quad\nt_i=\\frac{\\Delta_{1i}\\Delta_{23}}{\\Delta_{12}\\Delta_{13}}\n\\quad(i\\geq4).\n\\]\nThese are nonzero regular functions. With \\(B=(a_1,a_2)\\), left multiplication by\n\\[\nH=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;t_2\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}B^{-1}\n\\]\ngives by Cramer's rule\n\\[\nHa_1=(0,1)^t,\\quad\nHa_2=(t_2,0)^t,\\quad\nHa_3=(t_3,-t_3)^t,\\quad\nHa_i=(t_i,-t_iz_i)^t.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nQ=\\operatorname{row}(A_0(z)\\operatorname{diag}(t_i)).\n\\]\nConversely these ratios recover \\(z,t_2,\\ldots,t_m\\), proving inverse regular maps. The effective coefficient torus acts freely and transitively on the second factor.</p><p>As recorded in original Remark 7.2, before row changes the map from framed coefficients to \\(\\operatorname{Conf}_m(\\mathbb P^1)\\) is a principal \\((\\mathbb G_m)^m\\)-bundle: each coordinate covector retains one nonzero scalar. Row change \\(A\\mapsto gA\\) acts on the zero points by \\(g^{-t}\\). After row changes, it is the coefficient-torus quotient of \\(\\mathcal D_{2,m}\\), not \\(\\mathcal D_{2,m}\\) itself, that is \\(M_{0,m}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Coordinate roots and based marked comparison — original Lemma 7.5.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Coordinate roots and based marked comparison — original Lemma 7.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the normal form of Proposition 14, and put\n  \\[\n  Q_0(z)=\\operatorname{row}A_0(z),\\qquad P_0(z)=Q_0(z)^\\perp.\n  \\]\n  Then\n  \\[\n  Q(t,z)=\\operatorname{diag}(t_i)Q_0(z),\n  \\qquad\n  P(t,z)=\\operatorname{diag}(t_i^{-1})P_0(z).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal Y_0\\to M_m\\) be the normalized dual root-curve family. The row frame identifies \\(\\mathbb P^1\\simeq\\mathbb P(Q_0(z))\\); write\n  \\[\n  q_z:X_{Q_0(z)}\\to\\mathbb P^1,\n  \\qquad D_z=\\{\\infty,0,1,z_4,\\ldots,z_m\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  V_m=\\{(z,b)\\in M_m\\times\\mathbb P^1:b\\notin D_z\\}\\simeq M_{0,m+1}.\n  \\]\n  Let \\(B_{m,n}\\) be the complement of the coordinate boundary in \\(\\mathcal Y_0\\), with\n  \\[\n  \\beta:B_{m,n}\\to V_m,\\qquad(z,y)\\mapsto(z,q_z(y)).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The map \\(\\beta\\) is a connected finite étale \\(G_{m,n}\\)-torsor. Pullback of the root family to \\(B_{m,n}\\) has the tautological chosen lift \\(y\\) of \\(b=q_z(y)\\).</p><p>Fix an initial based equivariant identification with a root fiber, using orientation-preserving markings up to isotopy relative to labels. For every marked complex sphere with the deck datum in Lemma 15.1, there is a path in \\(V_m\\), lifted from the chosen point of \\(B_{m,n}\\), whose induced cohomological identification gives exactly the prescribed marked cover's endpoint Hodge filtration.</p><p>Consequently, a rational tensor remaining Hodge after root-family transport along every path in \\(M_m\\) is Hodge in every marked fiber.</p><p>The parameter space making coordinate-root and based-lift choices is\n\\[\n\\widetilde{\\mathcal D}_{2,m,n}\\times_{M_m}B_{m,n}\n\\simeq B_{m,n}\\times(\\mathbb G_m)^{m-1};\n\\]\nit is connected and projects smoothly and surjectively to \\(B_{m,n}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Away from \\(D_z\\), the covers in Lemma 15.1 are finite étale with free transitive phase fibers. Thus \\(\\beta\\) is a finite étale torsor.</p><p>The total \\(\\mathcal Y_0\\) is irreducible: it is smooth over irreducible \\(M_m\\), with connected fibers. Its irreducible components would be disjoint open components with nonempty open images; two images meet, contradicting connectedness of the fiber. Removing the boundary leaves an irreducible nonempty open. Hence \\(B_{m,n}\\) is connected.</p><p>Fix \\((z_0,y_0)\\), put \\(b_0=q_{z_0}(y_0)\\), and identify the labelled topological sphere and its cover with this based root fiber. The based epimorphisms agree on all ordered meridians. Changing the lift only conjugates the epimorphism and therefore does not change it for this abelian group.</p><p>For a prescribed marked complex sphere, normalize its first three branch labels by the unique Möbius map to \\(\\infty,0,1\\). The marking is an orientation-preserving reference-sphere homeomorphism \\(g\\) carrying every label to its target and fixing the first three at the endpoint. By Lemma 15.2, choose an unmarked isotopy \\(g_t\\) from identity to \\(g\\). Let \\(a_t\\) be the unique Möbius transformation taking \\(g_t(p_1),g_t(p_2),g_t(p_3)\\) to \\(\\infty,0,1\\). It varies continuously, and \\(a_0=a_1=\\mathrm{id}\\). Thus \\(a_tg_t\\) gives a path \\(\\gamma\\) in \\(V_m\\) with the exact prescribed endpoint marking.</p><p>Lift \\(\\gamma\\) uniquely to \\(B_{m,n}\\) from \\((z_0,y_0)\\), with upstairs points \\(y_t\\). Naturality of oriented labelled meridians and the abelianization description in Lemma 15.1 give equality of exact based epimorphisms at every \\(t\\). Covering-space lifting gives the unique equivariant lifted marking taking the fixed upstairs point to \\(y_t\\). Covering homotopy lifting and uniqueness make these continuous; fixed ramification power charts extend them to compactifications. They give a continuous trivialization along the interval and hence exactly the cohomological parallel transport along the projected path in \\(M_m\\).</p><p>At the endpoint this covering identification is holomorphic for the prescribed marked-cover complex structure: both are pulled back from the normalized base off branching, and agree in the local power charts. Their Hodge filtrations agree. The path need not lift to a loop; its endpoint deck phase is retained by \\(y_t\\). This proves the tensor consequence.</p><p>The map \\(V_m\\to M_m\\) is smooth surjective with punctured-line fibers; composing with the finite étale torsor remains smooth surjective. Lemma 15.1 gives the displayed fiber product and torus projection.</p><p>As in original Remark 7.6, the finite coordinate-root cover cannot generally be omitted: a torus coordinate need not have a regular \\(n\\)th root. Different choices differ by a phase. For\n\\[\nT_{\\mathrm{root}}=(\\mathbb G_m)^m/\\mathbb G_m,\n\\qquad\nT_{\\mathrm{coef}}=(\\mathbb G_m)^m/\\mathbb G_m,\n\\]\nthe induced map is \\([n]:T_{\\mathrm{root}}\\to T_{\\mathrm{coef}}\\), composed with inversion for the original intersection convention. Its kernel is \\(G_{m,n}\\). The coefficient action is free, while the root action retains exactly the phase stabilizer.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Normalized root family and fixed deck datum — original Proposition 7.3.** The map …", "statement": "<p>**Normalized root family and fixed deck datum — original Proposition 7.3.**</p><p>The map\n\\[\n\\rho:\\widetilde{\\mathcal D}_{2,m,n}\n:=M_m\\times(\\mathbb G_m)^{m-1}\\to\\mathcal D_{2,m},\n\\qquad\n(z,h_2,\\ldots,h_m)\\mapsto(z,h_2^n,\\ldots,h_m^n)\n\\]\nis a connected finite étale cover of degree \\(n^{m-1}\\). With \\(h_1=1\\), the maps\n\\[\n[y_i]\\mapsto[h_iy_i],\n\\qquad\n[x_i]\\mapsto[h_i^{-1}x_i]\n\\]\nidentify normalized dual and original root families with those for \\(Q(t,z),P(t,z)\\), and commute with \\(G_{m,n}\\).</p><p>Every \\(q_z\\) is a connected \\(G_{m,n}\\)-cover with the ordered branch points \\(D_z\\). Under\n\\[\nG_{m,n}\\simeq(\\mathbb Z/n)^m/\\langle(1,\\ldots,1)\\rangle\n\\]\nusing \\(\\zeta_n=\\exp(2\\pi i/n)\\), its ordered positive local monodromies are \\(\\overline e_1,\\ldots,\\overline e_m\\). The family runs over all ordered branch configurations with this fixed deck datum.</p>", "proof": "<p>The map is the \\(n\\)-power map on a torus times the identity. It is finite locally free of degree \\(n^{m-1}\\), étale since \\(nh_i^{n-1}\\) is a unit, and its source is irreducible. The coordinate scalings satisfy the equations for \\(P,Q\\), and their inverses and phase equivariance are immediate.</p><p>The normalized coordinate forms are\n\\[\nt,\\ s,\\ s-t,\\ s-z_4t,\\ldots,s-z_mt.\n\\]\nOn \\(t\\neq0\\), put \\(v=s/t\\), \\(w_i=y_i/y_1\\). The function field is\n\\[\nw_2^n=v,\\qquad w_3^n=v-1,\\qquad\nw_i^n=v-z_i\\quad(i\\geq4).\n\\]\nValuations at the distinct finite points make these functions independent in\n\\(\\mathbb C(v)^\\times/\\mathbb C(v)^{\\times n}\\). Kummer theory gives degree \\(n^{m-1}\\). Equivalently, successive adjunction uses Eisenstein at each new branch point, where earlier units have local roots. Thus the cover is connected and the phase group is its full deck group.</p><p>A positive meridian at finite point \\(i\\geq2\\) multiplies only \\(w_i\\) by \\(\\zeta_n\\), giving \\(\\overline e_i\\). At infinity every right side has a simple pole, so all \\(w_i\\), \\(i\\geq2\\), acquire \\(\\zeta_n^{-1}\\), giving\n\\[\n\\overline e_1=-\\sum_{i\\geq2}\\overline e_i.\n\\]\nThese elements have order \\(n\\), sum to zero, and generate the group.</p><p>The punctured-sphere epimorphism is exactly\n\\[\n\\pi_1(\\mathbb P^1\\setminus D_z,*)\n\\to H_1(\\mathbb P^1\\setminus D_z,\\mathbb Z/n)\n\\simeq(\\mathbb Z/n)^m/\\langle(1,\\ldots,1)\\rangle.\n\\]\nA pure orientation-preserving mapping class conjugates positive peripheral meridians and fixes this epimorphism. Classification of connected covers gives one fixed cover type. For each pointed complex structure, pullback charts off the punctures and the local ramification power charts give its unique compact-cover complex structure, realized by the root equations for every configuration.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Sphere isotopy — original Lemma 7.4.** Every orientation-preserving self-homeomorphism of \\(S^2\\) is isotopic to the identity through self-homeomorphisms, without requiring marked points to remain fixed.", "statement": "<p>**Sphere isotopy — original Lemma 7.4.**</p><p>Every orientation-preserving self-homeomorphism of \\(S^2\\) is isotopic to the identity through self-homeomorphisms, without requiring marked points to remain fixed.</p>", "proof": "<p>The annulus form of planar Schoenflies gives a disk-extension fact: if closed disks \\(D_0,D_1\\) lie in the interior of a closed disk \\(U\\), an orientation-preserving homeomorphism \\(D_0\\to D_1\\) extends to \\(U\\), fixed on \\(\\partial U\\). The complementary annuli admit an extension of the prescribed boundary circle maps. Orientation-preserving circle homeomorphisms are isotopic by linearly interpolating increasing lifts to \\(\\mathbb R\\); glue this annulus extension to the disk map.</p><p>For the given \\(g\\), choose \\(p\\), then \\(q\\neq p,g(p)\\), and a small disk \\(D\\) about \\(p\\) such that \\(D,g(D)\\) avoid \\(q\\). Their union lies in the interior of a closed disk \\(U\\subset S^2\\setminus\\{q\\}\\). Extend \\(g|_D\\) to \\(f\\) on \\(U\\), fixed on its boundary, then by identity outside \\(U\\). The Alexander trick isotopes \\(f\\) to identity: for a boundary-fixed unit-disk map \\(F\\),\n\\[\nF_t(x)=\n\\begin{cases}\ntF(x/t),&amp;|x|\\leq t,\\\\\nx,&amp;|x|\\geq t,\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad F_0=\\mathrm{id}.\n\\]\nNow \\(f^{-1}g\\) fixes \\(D\\) pointwise, so is boundary-fixed on the complementary disk. A second Alexander trick and composition give the required isotopy.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Algebraic very-generality for the dual curve — original Corollary 7.8.** Let \\(\\pi:\\mathcal Y_0\\to M_m\\) be the normalized dual root-curve family of Proposition 15.", "statement": "<p>**Algebraic very-generality for the dual curve — original Corollary 7.8.**</p><p>Let \\(\\pi:\\mathcal Y_0\\to M_m\\) be the normalized dual root-curve family of Proposition 15. There is a countable union \\(Z\\) of proper closed algebraic subsets of \\(M_m\\) such that, for \\(z\\notin Z\\), every CM abelian variety \\(B\\), and every \\(N\\geq1\\), the rational Hodge conjecture holds on\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\operatorname{Jac}(X_{Q_0(z)})\\times B\\bigr)^N.\n\\]\nThe same conclusion holds for the dual curve at every point of\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_{2,m}\\setminus\\lambda^{-1}(Z).\n\\]\nThe exceptional set is independent of the CM companion and the number of factors.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemma 16.1 to \\(\\mathbb V=R^1\\pi_*\\mathbb Z\\). At a remaining \\(z\\), choose an auxiliary basepoint and one of its lifts, obtaining a point of \\(B_{m,n}\\), and fix a based identification with the reference cover.</p><p>Every rational tensor Hodge at that marked fiber remains Hodge under all root-family path transports. Proposition 15 reaches every prescribed marked cover filtration by a based configuration path. Applying globality along its projection shows that the tensor is Hodge at every marked parameter. Thus the marked fiber lies in \\(\\mathcal T_\\varphi^{\\mathrm{Hg}}\\).</p><p>Proposition 9 and the cited saturated determinant criterion give the displayed Hodge-conjecture conclusions for every CM \\(B\\) and every \\(N\\).</p><p>The surjectivity of \\(\\lambda\\) makes the inverse image of each proper exceptional algebraic subset proper. At any complex point over \\(z\\), roots of its torus coordinates exist. Lemma 15.1 identifies its dual curve algebraically with \\(X_{Q_0(z)}\\), preserving the conclusion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Tensor-generic points — original Lemma 7.7.** Let \\(S\\) be a smooth connected complex algebraic variety carrying an integral polarized variation \\(\\mathbb V\\) of weight one.", "statement": "<p>**Tensor-generic points — original Lemma 7.7.**</p><p>Let \\(S\\) be a smooth connected complex algebraic variety carrying an integral polarized variation \\(\\mathbb V\\) of weight one. Mark its underlying local system on the universal cover of \\(S^{\\mathrm{an}}\\).</p><p>Outside a countable union of proper closed algebraic subsets of \\(S\\), every rational tensor in \\(\\mathbb V_s^{\\otimes2a}\\) of type \\((a,a)\\) remains of that type after parallel transport along every path starting at \\(s\\), for every \\(a\\geq0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The variation \\(\\mathbb V^{\\otimes2a}(a)\\), with tensor/Tate polarization rescaled by a positive integer if necessary, is integral polarized of weight zero. Denote its polarization by \\(Q_a\\). Clear denominators to make a rational tensor integral.</p><p>For an integral flat tensor \\(u\\) on the universal cover, let \\(H_u\\subset S\\) be the locus where some determination has type \\((0,0)\\). Cattani–Deligne–Kaplan [4, Corollary 1.3] makes \\(H_u\\) algebraic. Their proof writes it, for \\(K=Q_a(u,u)\\), as a union of images of connected components of the algebraic variety \\(S^{(K)}\\), finite over \\(S\\). There are finitely many such components and finite maps are closed, so \\(H_u\\) is closed.</p><p>If \\(u\\) is not globally Hodge on the universal cover, \\(H_u\\) is proper. On a simply connected analytic chart, its determinations are a countable set of integral tensors, each with closed analytic Hodge locus. For a real weight-zero tensor, type \\((0,0)\\) is membership in \\(F^0\\). None contains an open set, since analytic continuation and deck equivariance would make \\(u\\) globally Hodge. If \\(H_u=S\\), these nowhere-dense closed analytic loci cover the chart, contradicting Baire.</p><p>Remove \\(H_u\\) for all nonglobally-Hodge integral tensors in all powers. There are countably many. Any rational Hodge tensor at a remaining point, after clearing denominators, must be globally Hodge on the universal cover, equivalently Hodge after every path transport.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [2, 7, "cite"], [3, 2, "cite"], [3, 5, "cite"], [3, 7, "cite"], [3, 26, "cite"], [3, 27, "cite"], [4, 2, "cite"], [4, 7, "cite"], [5, 22, "cite"], [6, 7, "cite"], [7, 5, "cite"], [8, 0, "cite"], [8, 13, "cite"], [8, 15, "cite"], [8, 19, "cite"], [9, 13, "cite"], [9, 15, "cite"], [10, 13, "cite"], [10, 18, "cite"], [11, 10, "cite"], [12, 10, "cite"], [13, 19, "cite"], [13, 22, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"], [15, 19, "cite"], [15, 22, "cite"], [16, 15, "cite"], [16, 17, "cite"], [16, 18, "cite"], [17, 15, "cite"], [18, 15, "cite"], [19, 22, "cite"], [20, 19, "cite"], [21, 19, "cite"], [22, 0, "cite"], [22, 35, "cite"], [23, 1, "cite"], [23, 26, "cite"], [23, 30, "cite"], [24, 23, "cite"], [24, 25, "cite"], [25, 23, "cite"], [26, 28, "cite"], [26, 29, "cite"], [26, 30, "cite"], [27, 26, "cite"], [28, 29, "cite"], [29, 1, "cite"], [30, 29, "cite"], [31, 32, "cite"], [32, 35, "cite"], [33, 32, "cite"], [33, 35, "cite"], [34, 32, "cite"], [35, 1, "cite"], [36, 35, "cite"]]});
